var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"location":"guide/extending/#Adding-a-method","page":"Adding a method","title":"Adding a method","text":"A method package depends only on L2OCore. It defines a method type in one of the three families, declares the problem classes it supports, and overrides the pipeline steps that differ from the family fallback. Everything else (data generation, batched evaluation, samplers) comes from L2OCore.\n\nmodule L2OMyMethod\n\nimport L2OCore\nusing L2OCore: AbstractPrimalMethod, AbstractConvexClass, Prediction\n\nstruct MyMethod <: AbstractPrimalMethod\n    ρ::Float64\nend\n\n# 1. Problem classes the method applies to (default: none).\nL2OCore.supports(::MyMethod, ::AbstractConvexClass) = true\n\n# 2. Structure computed once per problem (default: predict everything).\nstruct MyDecomposition <: L2OCore.AbstractDecomposition\n    independent::Vector{Int}\n    # ... factorizations, index maps, ...\nend\nL2OCore.output_dimension(d::MyDecomposition) = length(d.independent)\nL2OCore.decompose(problem, ::MyMethod) = MyDecomposition(...)\n\n# 3. Completion: raw proxy output -> prediction (e.g. solve the equalities).\nfunction L2OCore.complete(::MyMethod, pipe, inputs, output)\n    X = ...                     # array operations only (AD- and GPU-friendly)\n    return Prediction(primal = X)\nend\n\n# 4. Training loss (default: supervised MSE against `targets`).\nfunction L2OCore.loss(m::MyMethod, pipe, inputs, output; kwargs...)\n    X = L2OCore.complete(m, pipe, inputs, output).primal\n    ev = pipe.evaluator\n    B = size(X, 2)\n    return sum(L2OCore.batch_objective(ev, X, inputs)) / B +\n           m.ρ * sum(abs2, L2OCore.row_violation(ev, X, inputs)) / B\nend\n\nend\n\nRules of thumb:\n\nType only the method argument of complete, loss, decompose and generate_data; the fallbacks leave the other arguments untyped so your methods are never ambiguous with them.\nAccept kwargs... in loss: training loops pass targets and proxy.\nWrite completions and losses with array operations on batches (batch = last dimension) so they can be differentiated and moved to a GPU.\nTest what the method's mathematics says on random instances, using evaluate and solver optima. For a completion that solves the equality constraints:\n\npred = complete(pipe, P, proxy(P))\nreport = evaluate(pipe, P, pred)\n@test maximum(report.max_equality_violation) <= 1e-8\ngap = abs.(report.objective .- data.objective) ./ max.(1, abs.(data.objective))","category":"section"},{"location":"guide/extending/#Wrapping-a-method","page":"Adding a method","title":"Wrapping a method","text":"Method-agnostic additions (for example derivative supervision) subtype AbstractMethodWrapper. The wrapper forwards everything to inner_method(w) and adds extra_loss(w, pipe, inputs, output; kwargs...) to the inner loss; it can attach derivative labels to datasets with with_derivatives.","category":"section"},{"location":"guide/extending/#Adding-an-evaluator-backend","page":"Adding a method","title":"Adding an evaluator backend","text":"Subtype AbstractEvaluator and implement batch_objective, constraint_values, row_violation, equality_rows, L2OCore.bound_rows, and, where relevant, cone_violation and dual_bound. Pass it to L2OPipeline(problem, method; evaluator = MyEvaluator(problem)); the tests of L2OCore compare backends against JuMPEvaluator on random points, and a new backend should do the same.","category":"section"},{"location":"guide/extending/#Adding-a-problem-class","page":"Adding a method","title":"Adding a problem class","text":"Subtype AbstractProblemClass (or AbstractConvexClass) and pass it as ParametricProblem(model; class = MyClass()).\n\n","category":"section"},{"location":"api/#LearningToOptimize-API","page":"LearningToOptimize","title":"LearningToOptimize API","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"api/#LearningToOptimize.FullyConnected-Tuple{Int64, Vector{Int64}, Int64}","page":"LearningToOptimize","title":"LearningToOptimize.FullyConnected","text":"FullyConnected(input_size::Int, hidden_sizes::Vector{Int}, output_size::Int)::FullyConnected\n\nCreate a fully connected neural network with input_size inputs, hidden_sizes hidden layers and output_size outputs. Adds a pass through layer for each layer.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#LearningToOptimize.PenaltyMethod","page":"LearningToOptimize","title":"LearningToOptimize.PenaltyMethod","text":"PenaltyMethod(; ρ_eq = 10.0, ρ_ineq = 10.0, squared = true)\n\nSelf-supervised penalty method: the reference primal method of the LearningToOptimize ecosystem, implemented only through the L2OCore interface.\n\nThe proxy x_θ(p) predicts all decision variables and is trained to minimize, over a distribution of parameters,\n\nmathcalL(θ) = mathbbE_pBig s fbig(x_θ(p) pbig)\n  + ρ_mathrmeq bigν_mathrmeqbig(x_θ(p) pbig)big\n  + ρ_mathrmineq bigν_mathrmineqbig(x_θ(p) pbig)big Big\n\nwhere s = 1 turns maximization into minimization, ν are the element-wise constraint violations returned by L2OCore.row_violation (equality rows and all other rows, respectively, plus distances to non-decomposable cones) and  is the squared 2-norm when squared = true and the 1-norm otherwise. With squared = true this is the soft loss of eq. (2) in Donti, Rolnick & Kolter, \"DC3: A learning method for optimization with hard constraints\" (ICLR 2021, arXiv:2104.12225), with λ_h = ρ_eq and λ_g = ρ_ineq; box rows (two-sided constraints, variable bounds) are penalized through max(l - f 0) + max(f - u 0), which is the mathrmReLU(g) of eq. (2) for each side.\n\nThe method supports the problem classes LinearConic and ConvexQuadratic, so the default MatrixEvaluator is used and the loss is differentiable with array AD. No solver is needed for training.\n\nPenalty proxies are in general infeasible; pair them with a completion or repair method when feasibility is required, and measure their violations with evaluate.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api/#LearningToOptimize.ProblemIterator","page":"LearningToOptimize","title":"LearningToOptimize.ProblemIterator","text":"ProblemIterator(ids::Vector{UUID}, pairs::Dict{VariableRef, Vector{Real}})\n\nIterator for optimization problem instances.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api/#LearningToOptimize.Recorder","page":"LearningToOptimize","title":"LearningToOptimize.Recorder","text":"Recorder(filename; primal_variables=[], dual_variables=[], filterfn=(model)-> termination_status(model) == MOI.OPTIMAL)\n\nRecorder of optimization problem solutions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api/#L2OCore.generate_data-Tuple{PenaltyMethod, Any}","page":"LearningToOptimize","title":"L2OCore.generate_data","text":"generate_data(::PenaltyMethod, pipe; sampler, n, rng, optimizer = nothing)\n\nPenalty training is self-supervised: only parameters are sampled. When an optimizer is given, instances are also solved so that the dataset carries reference optima for evaluation (objective gaps, dual bounds).\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#LearningToOptimize._objective_value-Tuple{JuMP.Model, Dict{JuMP.VariableRef, Float64}}","page":"LearningToOptimize","title":"LearningToOptimize._objective_value","text":"_objective_value(model::JuMP.Model, point::Dict{VariableRef,Float64})\n\nObjective value of a solution over a JuMP model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#LearningToOptimize.all_primal_variables-Tuple{JuMP.Model}","page":"LearningToOptimize","title":"LearningToOptimize.all_primal_variables","text":"all_primal_variables(model::JuMP.Model)\n\nReturns all primal variables of a JuMP model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#LearningToOptimize.box_sampler-Union{Tuple{F}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{JuMP.VariableRef, F}} where {T<:Real, F<:Integer}","page":"LearningToOptimize","title":"LearningToOptimize.box_sampler","text":"function box_sampler(\n    original_parameter::VariableRef,\n    num_s::F;\n    rng::AbstractRNG=Random.GLOBAL_RNG,\n    max_multiplier::T=1.25,\n    min_multiplier::T=0.8,\n) where {T<:Real,F<:Integer}\n\nUniformly sample values around the original parameter value over a discrete range inside a box.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#LearningToOptimize.feasibility_evaluator-Tuple{ProblemIterator, Any}","page":"LearningToOptimize","title":"LearningToOptimize.feasibility_evaluator","text":"feasibility_evaluator(problem_iterator::ProblemIterator, output)\n\nFeasibility evaluator of a solution over a ProblemIterator dataset. PS.: Probably can be made much more efficient.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#LearningToOptimize.general_evaluator-Tuple{ProblemIterator, Any}","page":"LearningToOptimize","title":"LearningToOptimize.general_evaluator","text":"general_evaluator(problem_iterator::ProblemIterator, model)\n\nGeneral evaluator of a predictor model over a ProblemIterator dataset. Returns objective, feasibility,  inference time and allocated memory.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#LearningToOptimize.general_sampler-Tuple{Vector{JuMP.VariableRef}}","page":"LearningToOptimize","title":"LearningToOptimize.general_sampler","text":"function general_sampler(\n    original_parameters::Vector{VariableRef};\n    samplers::Vector{Function}=[\n        (original_parameters) -> scaled_distribution_sampler(original_parameters, 1000),\n        LearningToOptimize.line_sampler, \n        (original_parameters) -> box_sampler(original_parameters, 10),\n    ]\n)\n\nThis function is a general sampler that uses a set of samplers to sample the parameter space.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#LearningToOptimize.general_sampler-Union{Tuple{AbstractString}, Tuple{T}} where T<:LearningToOptimize.FileType","page":"LearningToOptimize","title":"LearningToOptimize.general_sampler","text":"general_sampler(\n    file::AbstractString;\n    samplers::Vector{Function}=[\n        (original_parameters) -> scaled_distribution_sampler(original_parameters, 1000),\n        LearningToOptimize.line_sampler, \n        (original_parameters) -> box_sampler(original_parameters, 10),\n    ],\n    batch_id::UUID=uuid1(),\n    save_file::AbstractString=split(file, \".mof.json\")[1] * \"_input_\" * string(batch_id),\n    filetype::Type{T}=ArrowFile\n) where {T<:FileType}\n\nThis function is a general sampler that uses a set of samplers to sample the parameter space.  It loads the underlying model from file and samples the parameters. It saves the sampled parameters to save_file.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#LearningToOptimize.inconvexhull-Tuple{Matrix{Float64}, Matrix{Float64}, Any}","page":"LearningToOptimize","title":"LearningToOptimize.inconvexhull","text":"inconvexhull(training_set::Matrix{Float64}, test_set::Matrix{Float64}, solver; silent=true, tol=1e-4)\n\nCheck if new points are inside the convex hull of the given points. Solves a linear programming problem to check if the points are inside the convex hull.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#LearningToOptimize.line_sampler-Union{Tuple{F}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{Vector{T}, AbstractVector{F}}, Tuple{Vector{T}, AbstractVector{F}, AbstractVector{T}}} where {T<:Real, F<:Integer}","page":"LearningToOptimize","title":"LearningToOptimize.line_sampler","text":"function line_sampler(\n    original_parameters::Vector{T},\n    parameter_indexes::Vector{F},\n    range_p::AbstractVector{T},\n) where {T<:Real,F<:Integer}\n\nThis sampler returns a set of parameters that for a line in one dimension of the parameter space.  The idea is to change the value of one parameter and keep the rest constant.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#LearningToOptimize.load_parameters-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"LearningToOptimize","title":"LearningToOptimize.load_parameters","text":"load_parameters(file::AbstractString)\n\nLoad the parameters from a saved jump model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#LearningToOptimize.load_parameters-Tuple{JuMP.Model}","page":"LearningToOptimize","title":"LearningToOptimize.load_parameters","text":"load_parameters(model::JuMP.Model)\n\nLoad the parameters from a JuMP model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#LearningToOptimize.make_convex!-Tuple{Flux.PairwiseFusion}","page":"LearningToOptimize","title":"LearningToOptimize.make_convex!","text":"function make_convex!(chain::PairwiseFusion; tol = 1e-6)\n\nMake a PairwiseFusion model convex by making sure all dense layers (a part from the first) have positive weights. This procedure only makes sense for single output fully connected models.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#LearningToOptimize.make_convex!-Tuple{FullyConnected}","page":"LearningToOptimize","title":"LearningToOptimize.make_convex!","text":"function make_convex!(model::FullyConnected; tol = 1e-6)\n\nMake a FullyConnected model convex by making sure all dense layers (a part from the first) have positive weights. This procedure only makes sense for single output fully connected models.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#LearningToOptimize.model_outputs-Tuple{Function, ProblemIterator}","page":"LearningToOptimize","title":"LearningToOptimize.model_outputs","text":"model_outputs(model::Function, problem_iterator::ProblemIterator)\n\nReturns the output of a machine learning model over a ProblemIterator dataset.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#LearningToOptimize.model_outputs-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{T, ProblemIterator}} where T<:MLJBase.Machine","page":"LearningToOptimize","title":"LearningToOptimize.model_outputs","text":"model_outputs(mach::T, problem_iterator::ProblemIterator)\n\nReturns the output of a machine learning model over a ProblemIterator dataset.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#LearningToOptimize.objective_evaluator-Tuple{ProblemIterator, Any}","page":"LearningToOptimize","title":"LearningToOptimize.objective_evaluator","text":"objective_evaluator(problem_iterator::ProblemIterator, output)\n\nObjective evaluator of a solution over a ProblemIterator dataset. PS.: Probably can be made much more efficient.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#LearningToOptimize.record-Tuple{Recorder{ArrowFile}, Base.UUID}","page":"LearningToOptimize","title":"LearningToOptimize.record","text":"record(recorder::Recorder{ArrowFile}, id::UUID)\n\nRecord optimization problem solution to an Arrow file.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#LearningToOptimize.record-Tuple{Recorder{CSVFile}, Base.UUID}","page":"LearningToOptimize","title":"LearningToOptimize.record","text":"record(recorder::Recorder{CSVFile}, id::UUID)\n\nRecord optimization problem solution to a CSV file.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#LearningToOptimize.save-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{LearningToOptimize.AbstractProblemIterator, AbstractString, Type{T}}} where T<:LearningToOptimize.FileType","page":"LearningToOptimize","title":"LearningToOptimize.save","text":"save(problem_iterator::ProblemIterator, filename::AbstractString, file_type::Type{T})\n\nSave optimization problem instances to a file.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#LearningToOptimize.scaled_distribution_sampler-Union{Tuple{F}, Tuple{Vector{JuMP.VariableRef}, F}} where F<:Integer","page":"LearningToOptimize","title":"LearningToOptimize.scaled_distribution_sampler","text":"function scaled_distribution_sampler(\n    original_parameters::Vector{VariableRef},\n    num_s::F;\n    rng::AbstractRNG=Random.GLOBAL_RNG,\n    scaler_multiplier::Distribution=Uniform(0.8, 1.25),\n    distribution::Distribution=MvLogNormal(fill(-(1.05 .^ 2) ./ 2.0, length(original_parameters)), 1.05)\n) where {F<:Integer}\n\nSample from a distribution and scale the parameters by a random value over a uniform distribution.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#LearningToOptimize.solve_and_record-Tuple{ProblemIterator, Recorder, Integer}","page":"LearningToOptimize","title":"LearningToOptimize.solve_and_record","text":"solve_and_record(problem_iterator::ProblemIterator, recorder::Recorder, idx::Integer)\n\nSolve an optimization problem and record the solution.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#LearningToOptimize.solve_batch-Tuple{LearningToOptimize.AbstractProblemIterator, Any}","page":"LearningToOptimize","title":"LearningToOptimize.solve_batch","text":"solve_batch(problem_iterator::AbstractProblemIterator, recorder)\n\nSolve a batch of optimization problems and record the solutions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#LearningToOptimize.train_flux!-Tuple{L2OPipeline, Any, L2ODataset}","page":"LearningToOptimize","title":"LearningToOptimize.train_flux!","text":"train_flux!(pipe::L2OPipeline, model, data::L2ODataset; epochs = 100,\n            batchsize = 32, optimiser = Optimisers.Adam(1e-3),\n            rng = Random.default_rng(), callback = nothing) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nTrain a Flux model in place on pipe by minimizing loss(pipe, X, model(X); targets, proxy = model) with mini-batches of columns of data. Works for every method whose loss is differentiable with Zygote. Returns the mean training loss of each epoch. callback(epoch, loss) is called after every epoch.\n\nFor PenaltyMethod pipelines, the generic train_proxy!(method, pipe, model, data; kwargs...) calls train_flux!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#LearningToOptimize.update_model!-Tuple{JuMP.Model, Dict, Integer, Type{<:LearningToOptimize.AbstractParameterType}}","page":"LearningToOptimize","title":"LearningToOptimize.update_model!","text":"update_model!(model::JuMP.Model, pairs::Dict, idx::Integer)\n\nUpdate the values of parameters in a JuMP model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#LearningToOptimize.update_model!-Tuple{Type{LearningToOptimize.POIParamaterType}, JuMP.Model, JuMP.VariableRef, Any}","page":"LearningToOptimize","title":"LearningToOptimize.update_model!","text":"update_model!(model::JuMP.Model, p::VariableRef, val::Real)\n\nUpdate the value of a parameter in a JuMP model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#LearningToOptimize.variable_point-Tuple{Tables.MatrixTable, ProblemIterator, Int64}","page":"LearningToOptimize","title":"LearningToOptimize.variable_point","text":"variable_point(vals::Tables.MatrixTable, problem_iterator::ProblemIterator, idx::Int)\n\nReturns a dictionary with the values of the variables and parameters for the idx-th problem instance.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"api/#LearningToOptimize.variable_point-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Matrix{T}, ProblemIterator, Int64}} where T<:Real","page":"LearningToOptimize","title":"LearningToOptimize.variable_point","text":"variable_point(vals::Array{T,2}, problem_iterator::ProblemIterator, idx::Int)\n\nReturns a dictionary with the values of the variables and parameters for the idx-th problem instance.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"arrow/#**Reading-from-and-Compressing-Arrow-Files**","page":"Dataset generation: file formats","title":"Reading from and Compressing Arrow Files","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"arrow/#**Introduction**","page":"Dataset generation: file formats","title":"Introduction","text":"The package provides tools to work with Arrow files efficiently, allowing users to store and retrieve large datasets efficiently.\n\noutput_file = joinpath(save_path, \"$(case_name)_output_$(batch_id)\")\nrecorder = Recorder{filetype}(output_file)\nsuccessful_solves = solve_batch(problem_iterator, recorder)","category":"section"},{"location":"arrow/#**Compressing-Arrow-Files**","page":"Dataset generation: file formats","title":"Compressing Arrow Files","text":"Since appending data to Arrow files is slow and inefficient,  each instance of data is stored in a separate file. Therefore, in this case, the output files will look like this:\n\n<case_name>_output_<batch_id>_<instance_1_id>.arrow\n<case_name>_output_<batch_id>_<instance_2_id>.arrow\n...\n<case_name>_output_<batch_id>_<instance_n_id>.arrow\n\nLearningToOptimize.jl supports compressing batches of Arrow files for streamlined storage and retrieval.\n\nUse the LearningToOptimize.compress_batch_arrow function to compress a batch of Arrow files into a single file. This reduces disk usage and simplifies file management.\n\nFunction Signature:\n\nLearningToOptimize.compress_batch_arrow(\n    save_path,\n    case_name;\n    keyword_all = \"output\",\n    batch_id = string(batch_id),\n    # keyword_any = [string(batch_id)]\n)\n\nArguments:\nsave_path: Path to save the compressed file.\ncase_name: Name of the case or batch.\nkeyword_all: Filter files containing this keyword (default: \"output\").\nbatch_id: Identifier for the batch of files.\nkeyword_any: Array of keywords to further filter files.\n\nThe compressed file will be saved as <case_name>_output_<batch_id>.arrow.","category":"section"},{"location":"arrow/#**Reading-Arrow-Files**","page":"Dataset generation: file formats","title":"Reading Arrow Files","text":"Arrow files can be read using Julia’s Arrow library, which provides a tabular interface for data access.\n\nExample:\n\nusing Arrow\n\n# Read compressed Arrow file\ndata = Arrow.Table(\"<case_name>_output_<batch_id>.arrow\")\n\n# Access data as a DataFrame\nusing DataFrames\ndf = DataFrame(data)\n\nprintln(\"DataFrame content:\")\nprintln(df)\n\n","category":"section"},{"location":"design/#Design-of-the-LearningToOptimize-ecosystem","page":"Design","title":"Design of the LearningToOptimize ecosystem","text":"This page explains how the code is organized and why. The goal is that implementing a new learning-to-optimize (L2O) method takes only the code that is specific to that method. Data generation, evaluation of objectives, violations and dual bounds, samplers and dataset conventions are shared.","category":"section"},{"location":"design/#Packages","page":"Design","title":"Packages","text":"graph TD\n    subgraph \"LearningToOptimize.jl (this repository)\"\n        CORE[\"lib/L2OCore.jl<br/>interfaces, problem wrapper,<br/>evaluators, datasets\"]\n        MAIN[\"LearningToOptimize.jl<br/>penalty method, Flux training,<br/>dataset files, samplers, models\"]\n        DATA[\"lib/L2OData.jl (planned)<br/>dataset file formats\"]\n        KERN[\"lib/L2OKernels.jl (planned)<br/>batched GPU evaluators\"]\n    end\n    DLL[\"L2ODLL.jl<br/>dual Lagrangian learning\"]\n    DC3[\"L2ODC3.jl<br/>completion + correction\"]\n    ALM[\"L2OALM.jl<br/>primal-dual ALM\"]\n    LDF[\"LDF (planned)<br/>Lagrangian dual framework\"]\n    BNK[\"BatchNLPKernels.jl<br/>ExaModels kernels\"]\n    MAIN --> CORE\n    DATA -.-> CORE\n    KERN -.-> CORE\n    KERN -.-> BNK\n    DLL --> CORE\n    DC3 --> CORE\n    ALM --> CORE\n    LDF --> CORE\n    ALM -.-> BNK\n\npackage role dependencies\nL2OCore (lib/L2OCore) method hierarchy, problem classes, ParametricProblem, evaluators, datasets, method-dispatched pipeline JuMP, MathOptInterface, LinearAlgebra, SparseArrays, Random\nLearningToOptimize the penalty method, Flux training loop, existing dataset generation (ProblemIterator, recorders, samplers), FullyConnected models; re-exports L2OCore L2OCore + ML and file-format stack\nmethod packages (L2ODLL, L2ODC3, L2OALM, …) one method family member each L2OCore only (plus what the method itself needs)\nL2OKernels (planned) batched GPU evaluators as an AbstractEvaluator backend ExaModels / BatchNLPKernels, loaded as a package extension\n\nWhy a lib/ subpackage instead of a separate repository? The core and the reference method evolve together while the interface settles; one repository gives one CI run, one documentation site and atomic changes. Method packages live in their own repositories because they have their own authors, papers and dependency stacks.\n\nWhy so few dependencies in L2OCore? Method packages must be able to depend on it without inheriting Flux, MLJ, CSV or Arrow. Everything heavy is either in the main package or behind package extensions.","category":"section"},{"location":"design/#Subpackage-mechanism-and-Julia-versions","page":"Design","title":"Subpackage mechanism and Julia versions","text":"Julia ≥ 1.11 resolves unregistered local packages from a [sources] section in Project.toml; Julia 1.10 (the LTS) ignores that section. We support both:\n\nProject.toml declares [sources] L2OCore = {path = \"lib/L2OCore\"} (used on 1.11+);\nCI runs Pkg.develop(path = \"lib/L2OCore\") before testing on every version, which is what makes 1.10 work and is harmless on 1.11+;\nlib/L2OCore has its own Project.toml, test suite and CI job.\n\nOnce L2OCore is registered, [sources] and the develop step can be removed; [compat] L2OCore = \"0.1\" already pins the interface version.","category":"section"},{"location":"design/#Documentation-for-lib/-packages","page":"Design","title":"Documentation for lib/ packages","text":"There is one Documenter site. docs/make.jl loads both modules (modules = [LearningToOptimize, L2OCore]) and the API reference has one section per package, so cross-references between the core and the main package work and there is a single place to search.","category":"section"},{"location":"design/#Type-hierarchy","page":"Design","title":"Type hierarchy","text":"classDiagram\n    class AbstractL2OMethod\n    class AbstractPrimalMethod\n    class AbstractDualMethod\n    class AbstractPrimalDualMethod\n    class AbstractMethodWrapper\n    AbstractL2OMethod <|-- AbstractPrimalMethod\n    AbstractL2OMethod <|-- AbstractDualMethod\n    AbstractL2OMethod <|-- AbstractPrimalDualMethod\n    AbstractL2OMethod <|-- AbstractMethodWrapper\n    AbstractPrimalMethod <|-- PenaltyMethod\n    AbstractPrimalMethod <|-- DC3\n    AbstractPrimalMethod <|-- LDF\n    AbstractDualMethod <|-- DLL\n    AbstractPrimalDualMethod <|-- ALM\n\n    class AbstractProblemClass\n    class AbstractConvexClass\n    AbstractProblemClass <|-- AbstractConvexClass\n    AbstractConvexClass <|-- LinearConic\n    AbstractConvexClass <|-- ConvexQuadratic\n    AbstractProblemClass <|-- SmoothNonlinear\n    AbstractProblemClass <|-- MixedInteger\n\n    class AbstractEvaluator\n    AbstractEvaluator <|-- MatrixEvaluator\n    AbstractEvaluator <|-- JuMPEvaluator\n    AbstractEvaluator <|-- BatchKernelEvaluator_planned\n\nFamilies (AbstractPrimalMethod, AbstractDualMethod, AbstractPrimalDualMethod) select fallbacks: what is predicted, which labels are generated, how the raw output is split.\nProblem classes are traits of the problem, detected from the JuMP model (detect_problem_class) or given explicitly. They exist so that a method can declare which problems its mathematics applies to (supports).\nEvaluators are interchangeable backends for batched objective, constraint, violation and dual-bound evaluation.\nWrappers (AbstractMethodWrapper) add behavior to any method (e.g. derivative supervision) by forwarding everything to an inner method and adding a loss term.","category":"section"},{"location":"design/#Data-flow","page":"Design","title":"Data flow","text":"flowchart LR\n    M[\"JuMP model<br/>with MOI.Parameter\"] --> PP[\"ParametricProblem<br/>(layouts + class)\"]\n    PP --> PIPE[\"L2OPipeline(problem, method)<br/>supports? → decompose\"]\n    PIPE --> GD[\"generate_data<br/>(sampler, solver)\"]\n    GD --> DS[\"L2ODataset<br/>inputs, primal?, dual?,<br/>objective?, derivatives?\"]\n    DS --> TR[\"train proxy<br/>minimize loss(pipe, P, proxy(P))\"]\n    TR --> PX[\"proxy(P)\"]\n    PX --> CO[\"complete(pipe, P, raw)\"]\n    CO --> PR[\"Prediction(primal, dual)\"]\n    PR --> EV[\"evaluate → EvaluationReport<br/>objective, violations,<br/>dual bound, gap\"]\n\nLayouts are fixed once by ParametricProblem:\n\nquantity shape order\ninputs P num_parameters × B parameters(problem) (creation order)\nprimal X num_decision_variables × B decision_variables(problem)\ndual Y num_duals × B constraint_refs(problem), rows dual_layout(problem)[i], signs of JuMP.dual\n\nBatch-first (batch = last dimension) matches Flux, Lux and BatchNLPKernels and keeps every per-instance quantity a contiguous column.","category":"section"},{"location":"design/#Method-lifecycle","page":"Design","title":"Method lifecycle","text":"sequenceDiagram\n    participant U as user\n    participant P as L2OPipeline\n    participant M as method package\n    participant C as L2OCore fallbacks\n    U->>P: L2OPipeline(problem, method)\n    P->>M: supports(method, class)?\n    alt not supported\n        P-->>U: UnsupportedProblemError\n    end\n    P->>M: decompose(problem, method)\n    M-->>C: (fallback by family)\n    U->>P: generate_data(pipe; sampler, n)\n    P->>M: generate_data(method, pipe; …)\n    loop training\n        U->>P: loss(pipe, P, proxy(P); targets, proxy)\n        P->>M: loss(method, pipe, …)\n    end\n    U->>P: complete(pipe, P, proxy(P))\n    U->>P: evaluate(pipe, P, prediction)\n\nWhat a method package writes:\n\nfunction required? fallback\nstruct M <: Abstract*Method yes —\nsupports(::M, ::Class) = true yes, per class false (no silent fallback)\ndecompose(problem, ::M) if the method predicts a subset FullSpaceDecomposition by family\ngenerate_data(::M, pipe; …) if labels differ sample + solve, labels by family\ncomplete(::M, pipe, P, raw) if there is a completion/repair split raw output\nloss(::M, pipe, P, raw; …) if not supervised MSE supervised MSE\n\nWhy supports is opt-in. Each method rests on mathematics with hypotheses: DLL's dual completion needs a conic problem, DC3's equality completion needs an invertible Jacobian block. Running DLL on a nonconvex problem or DC3's correction on nonlinear constraints silently changes what the output means. The pipeline therefore refuses unsupported classes up front with an error. Users can still override a mis-detected class explicitly (ParametricProblem(model; class = …)).\n\nWhy the pipeline object. L2OPipeline checks support once, computes the decomposition once (which can be expensive: dualization, Jacobian factorizations) and holds the evaluator. It replaces hidden caches stored in model.ext. Extension functions take (method, pipe, …) so packages dispatch on their own method type; fallbacks leave the other arguments untyped so these definitions are never ambiguous.","category":"section"},{"location":"design/#Methods-at-a-glance","page":"Design","title":"Methods at a glance","text":"method family problem classes what the completion or loss does reference\nPenaltyMethod (this package) primal LinearConic, ConvexQuadratic soft loss, eq. (2) of the paper; outputs may be infeasible Donti et al. 2021\nDLL (L2ODLL.jl) dual conic (LinearConic, ConvexQuadratic) where, for every predicted y in the dual cone, the completion problem (8) is strictly feasible and bounded (e.g. finite variable bounds, strictly convex quadratic objective) completes y to a dual-feasible point, so its Lagrangian value is a valid dual bound (Theorems 1 and 2 of the paper) Tanneau & Van Hentenryck 2024; Klamkin et al. 2024\nDC3 (L2ODC3.jl) primal equality constraints with an invertible Jacobian block completion solves the equalities exactly; gradient correction reduces inequality violation (converging for linear constraints) Donti et al. 2021\nALM / PDL (L2OALM.jl) primal-dual smooth problems, kernels for batched evaluation augmented-Lagrangian loss with learned multipliers Park & Van Hentenryck 2023\nLDF (planned) primal smooth problems Lagrangian-dual loss with multipliers updated by subgradient steps Fioretto et al. 2020\n\nMethod packages test these properties numerically on random instances through evaluate (violations, dual bounds compared with solver optima).","category":"section"},{"location":"design/#Repair-layers","page":"Design","title":"Repair layers","text":"BoundedOutput and ProjectionRepair are AbstractMethodWrappers that work with any primal method: the first is an output layer used during training and inference that keeps predictions inside the variable bounds, the second a projection onto the feasible set at inference (convex classes only). Because they are wrappers, they forward the loss and completion to the inner method, leave dual predictions untouched, and tighten the support check (ProjectionRepair refuses non-convex classes even if the inner method accepts them).","category":"section"},{"location":"design/#Evaluation-layer,-front-ends-and-GPUs","page":"Design","title":"Evaluation layer, front ends and GPUs","text":"Every loss, completion and evaluation is expressed through the AbstractEvaluator functions (batch_objective, constraint_values, row_violation, cone_violation, dual_bound). Two backends ship today:\n\nMatrixEvaluator: extracts A, B, b, Q, c, H once; every batch operation is a sparse-dense product or broadcast. It is differentiable with array AD and element-type generic. Moving its arrays to a GPU (Adapt) is the planned L2OCore extension for linear/quadratic problems.\nJuMPEvaluator: per-instance reference through JuMP.value; it is the ground truth in tests, never used in training loops.\n\nThe plan for nonlinear problems and for an ExaModels front end is a single kernel backend:\n\nflowchart LR\n    J[\"JuMP model<br/>+ MOI.Parameter\"] -->|\"JuMP → ExaModels<br/>(function-level conversion)\"| E[\"parametric ExaModel\"]\n    X[\"ExaModels model<br/>(user-written)\"] --> E\n    E --> BM[\"BatchNLPKernels.BatchModel\"]\n    BM --> KE[\"kernel evaluator<br/>(AbstractEvaluator, package extension)\"]\n    KE --> L[\"losses / completions / evaluation<br/>on CPU or GPU\"]\n\nProblems stated in JuMP or directly in ExaModels end up as a parametric ExaModel; a BatchModel from BatchNLPKernels provides batched objective, constraints, Jacobian products and violations with ChainRulesCore rules.\nA kernel evaluator implementing the AbstractEvaluator functions on top of a BatchModel plugs into every method unchanged. It lives in a package extension (L2OCore + BatchNLPKernels), keeping the core light.\nAn ExaModels front end subtypes AbstractParametricProblem (which ParametricProblem already does), implementing the layout functions, objective_sign and default_evaluator.\nBatched solvers can label data much faster than a loop over optimize!; a batched-modeling layer (batched parameters/constraints over JuMP, with a bridge to ExaModels and NLPModels) would let solve_instances hand a whole batch to such solvers. This is tracked as a follow-up issue.\n\nCI runs on CPU. GPU paths are written without scalar indexing and are testable by running the same tests with arrays adapted to the device.","category":"section"},{"location":"design/#Machine-learning-frameworks","page":"Design","title":"Machine-learning frameworks","text":"L2OCore is framework-agnostic: a proxy is any callable on a batch. The main package ships a Flux training loop (train_flux!) because Flux is already a dependency. Derivative (Sobolev) supervision needs nested AD: an input-Jacobian product of the proxy inside the loss, then a gradient with respect to the weights. We measured which combinations give correct gradients (compared with central finite differences) on a two-layer network:\n\nproxy outer AD inner AD (input VJP) result\nLux (StatefulLuxLayer) Zygote Lux.vector_jacobian_product(…, AutoZygote(), …) correct\nLux (StatefulLuxLayer) ForwardDiff Zygote correct\nLux (StatefulLuxLayer) Zygote Zygote (Zygote.gradient inside the loss) error (array mutation)\nLux Enzyme Enzyme error (runtime activity / mutability)\nFlux Zygote Zygote correct\nFlux ForwardDiff Zygote correct\nLux or Flux Zygote ForwardDiff silently wrong (nothing gradient)\n\nMeasured with Lux 1.32, Flux 0.16, Zygote 0.7, ForwardDiff 1.5 and a recent Enzyme on a Dense(3→8, tanh) → Dense(8→2) network with Float64 weights (Float32 weights make the finite-difference reference unreliable); reproduce with julia dev/nested_ad_matrix.jl. The table is evidence for the configurations listed, not a general statement; method packages test each framework on the models they ship examples for.\n\nDecision:\n\nmethod packages must work with any callable proxy; L2OCore never differentiates through the proxy itself;\nLux and Flux are both supported for losses that need nested AD, each in the configurations the tests show to be correct:\nLux through its vector_jacobian_product / jacobian_vector_product helpers under an outer Zygote gradient (Lux maintains these for exactly this use, and they also work on GPUs); nesting Zygote.gradient by hand inside a Lux loss fails, so derivative-supervision code calls the helpers;\nFlux (a Chain, or any Zygote-differentiable callable) with Zygote both inside the loss and for the outer gradient with respect to the weights;\nthe Flux loop in the main package (train_flux!) covers first-order losses (penalty, supervised, augmented-Lagrangian losses); a method package that needs derivative supervision provides its own loss for either framework;\nnever put ForwardDiff inside a Zygote-differentiated loss for parameter gradients: Zygote treats the inner call as constant and returns nothing without an error.","category":"section"},{"location":"design/#Datasets-and-trained-models-on-the-Hugging-Face-Hub","page":"Design","title":"Datasets and trained models on the Hugging Face Hub","text":"Datasets and trained proxies are shared under huggingface.co/LearningToOptimize. Proposed conventions (design only):\n\nDataset card fields\n\nfield example why\nproblem_id, problem_version pglib_opf_case5_pjm/DCPPowerModel, 1.0 reproducible problem identity\nproblem_file .mof.json with parameters the JuMP model, readable by MOI.FileFormats\nparameter_sampler {\"type\": \"ScaledSampler\", \"low\": 0.8, \"high\": 1.2} (#32) inputs distribution, needed to generate more data\nlayouts names of parameters, variables, constraints in order ties columns to model entities\nsolver, solver_version, tolerances HiGHS 1.x, primal_feas = 1e-7 label quality\nlabels primal, dual, objective, status, optional `derivatives: pullback jacobian`\nlabel_quality max violation ≤ 1e-6, dual gap ≤ 1e-6 measured with evaluate before upload\nsplits, seed train/val/test, 42 comparability\n\nModel card fields: method (type and hyper-parameters), proxy (architecture, framework, parameter count), training_data (dataset id and revision), metrics (EvaluationReport means on the test split, gap, max violation, dual bound where the method predicts duals).","category":"section"},{"location":"design/#Compatibility-with-the-existing-API","page":"Design","title":"Compatibility with the existing API","text":"All existing exports (ProblemIterator, Recorder, ArrowFile, CSVFile, samplers, FullyConnected, evaluators) are unchanged. L2OCore names are re-exported. Names that collide with other packages commonly loaded together (MLJ.evaluate) are resolved inside LearningToOptimize in favor of the L2OCore meaning; users that load MLJ too can write LearningToOptimize.evaluate.","category":"section"},{"location":"design/#Planned-work","page":"Design","title":"Planned work","text":"Move ProblemIterator, recorders and file formats to lib/L2OData.jl once L2ODataset I/O is designed (#12), so the main package can drop Arrow/CSV/DataFrames for users who only train.\nKernel evaluator extension and the ExaModels front end (#33).\nGraph encoders from the problem structure (#29).\n\n","category":"section"},{"location":"theory/#Theory","page":"Theory","title":"Theory","text":"This page fixes the notation used across the ecosystem and states the mathematics behind the evaluators, the Lagrangian dual bound and the penalty method, with references to the papers they come from.","category":"section"},{"location":"theory/#Parametric-optimization","page":"Theory","title":"Parametric optimization","text":"A parametric problem maps a parameter vector p in mathbbR^n_p to an instance\n\nmathrmOPT(p) = min_x in mathbbR^n  f(x p)\nquad textst quad g_i(x p) in mathcalS_i quad i = 1 dots m\ntagT1\n\nwhere each mathcalS_i is a MathOptInterface set (EqualTo, GreaterThan, Interval, Nonnegatives, SecondOrderCone, …). In JuMP, p are variables in MOI.Parameter, x are all other variables (decision_variables), and the constraints, including variable bounds, are constraint_refs (in this order). Maximization is handled by the sign s = textttobjective_sign(problem) in 1 -1: s f is minimized.\n\nA proxy phi_theta maps a batch of parameters P = p_1 dots p_B to raw outputs; a method's completion turns raw outputs into a prediction (hat X hat Y) of primal and/or dual solutions. Training minimizes a method-specific loss mathbbE_p sim mathcalD ell(phi_theta(p) p) over a parameter distribution mathcalD (an AbstractParameterSampler).","category":"section"},{"location":"theory/#Matrix-form","page":"Theory","title":"Matrix form","text":"For the classes LinearConic and ConvexQuadratic, with parameters entering affinely, MatrixEvaluator represents (T1) as\n\nmin_x  tfrac12 x^top Q x + c^top x + x^top H p + c_p^top p + c_0\nquad textst quad A x + B p + b in mathcalK = mathcalK_1 times dots times mathcalK_m \ntagT2\n\nBox-like sets are written as row bounds l le A x + B p + b le u (l_r = u_r for equalities, infinite entries allowed); other cones stay as blocks. Violations are\n\nnu_r(x p) = max(l_r - a_r^top x - b_r^top p - b_r^0 0) + max(a_r^top x + b_r^top p + b_r^0 - u_r 0)\nqquad\nnu_k^mathrmcone(xp) = operatornamedist(A_k x + B_k p + b_k mathcalK_k)\ntagT3\n\nMatrixEvaluator evaluates (T2) and (T3) for a whole batch with sparse products; on any (X P) its objective, constraint values and violations equal those of the per-instance JuMPEvaluator, which the tests compare on random points.","category":"section"},{"location":"theory/#Lagrangian-dual-bound","page":"Theory","title":"Lagrangian dual bound","text":"Dual variables follow the MathOptInterface convention returned by JuMP.dual (MOI documentation, \"Duality\"): for min a_0^top x + b_0 s.t. A_i x + b_i in mathcalC_i the dual is max -sum_i b_i^top y_i + b_0 s.t. a_0 - sum_i A_i^top y_i = 0, y_i in mathcalC_i^*; for maximization the dual is min sum_i b_i^top y_i + b_0 s.t. a_0 + sum_i A_i^top y_i = 0, with the same cones. Hence the signs of the duals depend only on the sets, not on the objective sense: y_k in mathcalK_k^* for conic blocks, and for a box row y_r ge 0 if only the lower side is finite, y_r le 0 if only the upper side is finite, free otherwise. An Interval row (not a cone) is treated as the pair f ge l (multiplier max(y_r0)) and f le u (multiplier min(y_r0)), which is the sign convention solvers report for its ConstraintDual (strong duality holds with this convention on a QP with an Interval row, which the tests use as a reference). Writing f_r = a_r^top x + b_r^top p + b^0_r for the function value of row r (all rows, box and conic), the Lagrangian of the minimization form (objective s f) is\n\nL(x y p) = s f(x p) - sum_r y_r f_r\n  + sum_r in textbox big(max(y_r 0) l_r + min(y_r 0) u_rbig)\ntagT4\n\nFor a box row the last two terms equal -max(y_r0)(f_r - l_r) - min(y_r0)(f_r - u_r); for a conic block they reduce to -y_k^top f_k. For maximization, -L is exactly the MOI Lagrangian f + sum_i y_i^top (A_i x + b_i), which is why the minimization form uses the same y.\n\nand the dual function is d(y p) = inf_x L(x y p). With r(y p) = s(c + H p) - A^top y:\n\nif Q = 0 (LP/conic; this is the MOI dual objective above): ``d(y;p) = s(cp^\\top p + c0) - y^\\top(Bp + b)\n\\text{box terms}whenr(y;p) = 0 and-\\infty`` otherwise;\nif sQ succeq 0: ``d(y;p) = s(cp^\\top p + c0) - y^\\top(Bp + b)\n\\text{box terms} - \\tfrac12 r^\\top (sQ)^{+} rwhenr \\in\n\\operatorname{range}(sQ) and-\\inftyotherwise (for invertibleQthis is the MOI dual of a QP-\\tfrac12 z^\\top Q^{-1} z + b0 - \\sumi yi^\\top biwithz = -r``);\nif sQ is not positive semidefinite, d(y p) = -infty for every y, so dual_bound returns -infty (+infty for maximization), even when the class was declared ConvexQuadratic by hand.\n\ndual_bound(evaluator, Y, P) returns s d(yp) together with the dual infeasibility (largest of the stationarity residual, sign violations and dual-cone distances).\n\nWeak duality. If y is dual feasible (signs and dual cones as above, and r(yp) = 0 or r in operatornamerange(sQ)), then d(y p) le smathrmOPT(p): the returned bound is le mathrmOPT(p) for minimization and ge for maximization. No convexity is needed: for any feasible x, every subtracted term in (T4) is ge 0 by the sign conventions, so L(xyp) le s f(xp). Since dual feasibility does not depend on right-hand-side parameters, the optimal duals of one instance give a bound at every other instance with the same matrices; for a strictly convex QP every sign-feasible y is dual feasible because operatornamerange(sQ) is everything. The tests use both facts on random instances.\n\nTolerance. Numerically, r is accepted when r_infty le atol (or the range residual is). The bound then holds up to the residual: since L is affine in x along the residual, d(yp) le smathrmOPT(p) + r_infty x^star_1 for any optimal x^star. Methods that need exact bounds (DLL) complete y so that r = 0 holds by construction.\n\nDual completion (DLL). The DLL paper (Tanneau and Van Hentenryck,\n\nwrites the primal as min c^top x s.t. ``Ax \\succeq_{\\mathcal K}\n\nbHx \\succeq{\\mathcal C} h(its eq (6)) and the dual as\\max b^\\top y + h^\\top zstA^\\top y + H^\\top z = cy \\in \\mathcal K^z \\in \\mathcal C^(eq (7)) withf = Ax - b`this is exactly the MOI form above, sodualboundof a completed DLL prediction is the boundb^\\top \\hat y + h^\\top \\hat zof eq. (7a). Its Theorem 1 assumes that for every\\hat y \\in \\mathcal K^*the inner problem (8) is strictly feasible inHx \\succ_{\\mathcal C} hand bounded; then a completion\\hat zexists and(\\hat y, \\hat z)is dual feasible (Theorem 2: the optimal completion gives the strongest bound for fixed\\hat y). For finite variable bounds (its Example 1) the completion is\\hat z^l = |c - A^\\top \\hat y|^+,\\hat z^u = |c - A^\\top \\hat y|^-(\\hat z^u \\le 0in MOI signs). The tests apply this completion to random\\hat yand compare the bound with the solver optimum;\\hat y = y^\\star` recovers the optimum.\n\nStrong duality. For a convex problem with a finite optimal value (LPs and convex QPs with affine constraints; for general cones under a constraint qualification such as Slater's condition), the bound at a primal-dual optimal pair equals the optimal value, up to solver tolerance. The tests compare the two on an LP (min and max), a convex QP (min, and concave max), a QP with a singular PSD Hessian and an SOCP.","category":"section"},{"location":"theory/#Measuring-a-prediction","page":"Theory","title":"Measuring a prediction","text":"evaluate(pipe, P, prediction) returns an EvaluationReport with, per instance, the objective f(hat x p), the largest violation over all constraints (rows and cones, (T3)), the largest violation over equality rows (l_r = u_r) and over the other constraints, and, for dual predictions, sd(hat y p) and the dual infeasibility. When both a primal and a dual prediction are present it also reports the relative gap f(hat xp) - sd(hat yp)  max(1 f(hat xp)). Comparing the objective with solver optima gives the optimality gap of a primal proxy.","category":"section"},{"location":"theory/#The-penalty-method","page":"Theory","title":"The penalty method","text":"The reference method minimizes the soft loss of eq. (2) in Donti, Rolnick and Kolter (2021):\n\nell_mathrmsoft(hat x p) = s f(hat x p)\n  + rho_mathrmeq sum_r l_r = u_r nu_r(hat x p)^2\n  + rho_mathrmineq Big(sum_r l_r  u_r nu_r(hat x p)^2 + sum_k nu_k^mathrmcone(hat xp)^2Big)\ntagT5\n\nFor a box row, nu_r is the ReLU of both one-sided constraints, so (T5) is eq. (2) of that paper with lambda_h = rho_mathrmeq and lambda_g = rho_mathrmineq; squared = false uses the 1-norm instead. Deviations: none in the formula. Variable bounds are inequalities g of eq. (2) like any other, and distances to non-decomposable cones (e.g. second-order cones) play the role of mathrmReLU(g) for conic constraints g(x) in mathcal K, which eq. (2) does not cover. The loss is averaged over the batch (the expectation in the training objective).\n\nAt a feasible point the penalty terms vanish and the loss equals the mean of s f (also for maximization). The tests compare the loss with eq. (2) written out by hand for a small LP, and its Zygote gradient with central finite differences on an LP and on an SOCP (where the cone distance is active).\n\nAs the DC3 paper notes, soft losses do not enforce the constraints and can give infeasible outputs; use a method with a completion step when feasibility matters.","category":"section"},{"location":"theory/#Repair-layers","page":"Theory","title":"Repair layers","text":"Repair layers are method wrappers: they change how a raw prediction is turned into a completed one, and therefore which constraints the completed prediction satisfies, independently of the method that trained the proxy.\n\nBounded output. BoundedOutput(inner; mode) maps the primal block z of the raw output into the box l le x le u declared on the variables, before both the loss and the completion of the inner method:\n\nbeta(z)_j = begincases\nl_j + (u_j - l_j)sigma(z_j)  l_j u_j text finite \nl_j + operatornamesoftplus(z_j)  textonly  l_j text finite \nu_j - operatornamesoftplus(-z_j)  textonly  u_j text finite \nz_j  textfree\nendcases\nqquadtextorqquad\nbeta(z) = min(max(z l) u) = Pi_lu(z)\ntagT6\n\nBy construction l le beta(z) le u for every z. The smooth map is strictly increasing in each coordinate, so it has no zero-gradient region; the clamp is the Euclidean projection onto the box (the identity on the box, and nonexpansive) but its gradient vanishes outside the box. Changing x can break other primal constraints of the inner method (e.g. equalities), while dual predictions, and hence their dual bounds, are untouched.\n\nProjection repair. ProjectionRepair(inner, optimizer) replaces the primal prediction hat x by\n\nPi_mathcal F(p)(hat x) = argmin_x in mathcal F(p) x - hat x_2^2\ntagT7\n\nsolved per instance by a convex solver at inference; training is unchanged. For a convex class with mathcal F(p) neq emptyset, mathcal F(p) is closed and convex, so (T7) has a unique solution, which is feasible up to the solver's tolerance. The projection onto a closed convex set is (firmly) nonexpansive (see, e.g., Bauschke and Combettes, Convex Analysis and Monotone Operator Theory in Hilbert Spaces, Ch. 4), so for every feasible x, in particular every optimal x^star, Pi(hat x) - x le hat x - x: repair never moves a prediction away from the optimum. Feasible predictions are unchanged (to solver accuracy). The objective value need not improve. Projection layers are one of the remedies for infeasible proxies discussed in the survey of Kotary et al. (2021).","category":"section"},{"location":"theory/#References","page":"Theory","title":"References","text":"H. H. Bauschke, P. L. Combettes. Convex Analysis and Monotone Operator Theory in Hilbert Spaces. Springer, 2nd ed., 2017.\nP. L. Donti, D. Rolnick, J. Z. Kolter. DC3: A learning method for optimization with hard constraints. ICLR 2021. arXiv:2104.12225.\nJ. Kotary, F. Fioretto, P. Van Hentenryck, B. Wilder. End-to-End Constrained Optimization Learning: A Survey. IJCAI 2021. arXiv:2103.16378.\nM. Tanneau, P. Van Hentenryck. Dual Lagrangian Learning for Conic Optimization. NeurIPS 2024. arXiv:2402.03086.\nM. Klamkin, M. Tanneau, P. Van Hentenryck. Dual Interior-Point Optimization Learning. arXiv:2402.02596.\nS. Park, P. Van Hentenryck. Self-Supervised Primal-Dual Learning for Constrained Optimization. AAAI 2023. arXiv:2208.09046.\nF. Fioretto, P. Van Hentenryck, T. W. K. Mak, C. Tran, F. Baldo, M. Lombardi. Lagrangian Duality for Constrained Deep Learning. ECML-PKDD 2020. arXiv:2001.09394.\n\n","category":"section"},{"location":"parametertype/#**Parameter-Types-for-Optimization-Problems**","page":"Dataset generation: parameter types","title":"Parameter Types for Optimization Problems","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"parametertype/#**Introduction**","page":"Dataset generation: parameter types","title":"Introduction","text":"When working with optimization problems, LearningToOptimize.jl supports multiple parameterization strategies. These strategies influence the behavior and performance of the solver and allow flexibility in retrieving information such as dual variables.\n\n","category":"section"},{"location":"parametertype/#**Comparison**","page":"Dataset generation: parameter types","title":"Comparison","text":"Parameter Type Supported Problems Dual Support Performance Notes\nJuMPParameterType All Yes Moderate General-purpose; slower.\nJuMPNLPParameterType NLP Only No Fast Optimized for NLP problems.\nPOIParameterType (Default) Linear, Conic Yes Fast Requires POI-wrapped solvers.\n\n","category":"section"},{"location":"parametertype/#**Supported-Parameter-Types**","page":"Dataset generation: parameter types","title":"Supported Parameter Types","text":"POIParameterType (Default):\nDescription:\nExtends MOI.Parameters for linear and conic problems.\nCompatible with solvers wrapped using ParametricOptInterface.\nSupports fetching duals w.r.t. parameters.\nLimitations:\nNot compatible with nonlinear solvers.\nUsage Example: Default behavior when using ProblemIterator without specifying param_type.\nJuMPParameterType:\nDescription:\nAdds a variable as a parameter with an additional constraint during solve_batch.\nSlower compared to other types but supports fetching duals w.r.t. the parameter.\nCompatible with all problem types.\nUsage Example:\nusing JuMP, LearningToOptimize\n\nmodel = JuMP.Model(HiGHS.Optimizer)\n@variable(model, x)\np = @variable(model, _p)\n@constraint(model, cons, x + _p >= 3)\n@objective(model, Min, 2x)\n\nnum_p = 10\nproblem_iterator = ProblemIterator(\n    Dict(p => collect(1.0:num_p));\n    param_type = LearningToOptimize.JuMPParameterType\n)\n\nrecorder = Recorder{ArrowFile}(\"output.arrow\"; primal_variables = [x], dual_variables = [cons])\nsolve_batch(problem_iterator, recorder)\nAdvantages:\nWorks with all solvers and problem types.\nDuals w.r.t. parameters are available.\nJuMPNLPParameterType:\nDescription:\nUtilizes MOI’s internal parameter structure.\nOptimized for speed but limited to nonlinear programming (NLP) problems.\nDoes not support fetching duals w.r.t. parameters.\nUsage Example:\nusing JuMP, LearningToOptimize\n\nmodel = JuMP.Model(Ipopt.Optimizer)\n@variable(model, x)\np = @variable(model, _p in MOI.Parameter(1.0))\n@constraint(model, cons, x + _p >= 3)\n@objective(model, Min, 2x)\n\nnum_p = 10\nproblem_iterator = ProblemIterator(\n    Dict(p => collect(1.0:num_p));\n    param_type = LearningToOptimize.JuMPNLPParameterType\n)\n\nrecorder = Recorder{ArrowFile}(\"output.arrow\"; primal_variables = [x], dual_variables = [cons])\nsolve_batch(problem_iterator, recorder)\nAdvantages:\nFast and efficient for NLP problems.\nNo external wrappers required.\n\n","category":"section"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#L2OCore-API","page":"L2OCore","title":"L2OCore API","text":"L2OCore lives in lib/L2OCore of this repository. All of its exported names are re-exported by LearningToOptimize.","category":"section"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#Methods-and-proxies","page":"L2OCore","title":"Methods and proxies","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#Problems-and-classes","page":"L2OCore","title":"Problems and classes","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#Evaluation","page":"L2OCore","title":"Evaluation","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#Data","page":"L2OCore","title":"Data","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#Pipeline","page":"L2OCore","title":"Pipeline","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#Repair-layers","page":"L2OCore","title":"Repair layers","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#L2OCore.L2OCore","page":"L2OCore","title":"L2OCore.L2OCore","text":"L2OCore\n\nLightweight interfaces shared by every Learning-to-Optimize (L2O) method in the LearningToOptimize ecosystem.\n\nL2OCore defines what an L2O method is (a type in a small hierarchy), which problems it accepts (supports), how its predictions are evaluated (evaluate) and the method-dispatched pipeline\n\nParametricProblem ─ decompose ─ generate_data ─ (train proxy) ─ complete ─ evaluate\n\nwith sane fallbacks for each method family. It depends only on JuMP, MathOptInterface and standard libraries, so method packages can depend on it without pulling in a machine-learning framework.\n\nConventions:\n\nBatch-first: the batch is always the last dimension. Parameters are num_parameters × B, primal predictions num_decision_variables × B, dual predictions num_duals × B.\nElement-type generic: evaluators are parametrized by the element type.\nMinimal scalar indexing in vectorized evaluation code so arrays can be moved to accelerators by a future kernels extension.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"module"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#L2OCore.AbstractL2OMethod","page":"L2OCore","title":"L2OCore.AbstractL2OMethod","text":"AbstractL2OMethod\n\nSupertype of every Learning-to-Optimize method.\n\nA method is a (usually small, immutable) struct holding its hyper-parameters. Concrete methods subtype one of the three families AbstractPrimalMethod, AbstractDualMethod or AbstractPrimalDualMethod; the family selects the fallback behavior of decompose, generate_data, loss and complete.\n\nTo add a method, a package defines\n\nstruct MyMethod <: L2OCore.AbstractPrimalMethod\n    ρ::Float64\nend\nL2OCore.supports(::MyMethod, ::L2OCore.LinearConic) = true\n\nand overrides only the pipeline steps that differ from the family fallback.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#L2OCore.AbstractPrimalMethod","page":"L2OCore","title":"L2OCore.AbstractPrimalMethod","text":"AbstractPrimalMethod <: AbstractL2OMethod\n\nMethods whose proxy predicts (a subset of) the primal decision variables, e.g. penalty methods, DC3, LDF.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#L2OCore.AbstractDualMethod","page":"L2OCore","title":"L2OCore.AbstractDualMethod","text":"AbstractDualMethod <: AbstractL2OMethod\n\nMethods whose proxy predicts (a subset of) the dual variables, e.g. Dual Lagrangian Learning (DLL). A dual feasible prediction gives a valid bound on the optimal value through dual_bound.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#L2OCore.AbstractPrimalDualMethod","page":"L2OCore","title":"L2OCore.AbstractPrimalDualMethod","text":"AbstractPrimalDualMethod <: AbstractL2OMethod\n\nMethods that predict both primal and dual variables, e.g. primal-dual learning with an augmented Lagrangian (ALM/PDL).\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#L2OCore.AbstractMethodWrapper","page":"L2OCore","title":"L2OCore.AbstractMethodWrapper","text":"AbstractMethodWrapper <: AbstractL2OMethod\n\nA method that wraps another method and changes only part of its behavior — the hook for method-agnostic add-ons such as derivative (Sobolev) supervision or extra regularizers. A wrapper must implement\n\nL2OCore.inner_method(w) → the wrapped method;\nL2OCore.extra_loss(w, pipe, inputs, output; targets, proxy, kwargs...) → the additional loss term (a scalar).\n\nLeave the pipe, inputs and output arguments untyped (or typed no more narrowly than the fallbacks) to avoid method ambiguities. Everything else (family, supports, decompose, generate_data, complete) is forwarded to the inner method, and loss is loss(inner) + extra_loss(wrapper). A wrapper may override any of them, e.g. generate_data to attach derivative labels with with_derivatives.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#L2OCore.inner_method","page":"L2OCore","title":"L2OCore.inner_method","text":"inner_method(w::AbstractMethodWrapper) -> AbstractL2OMethod\n\nThe method wrapped by w. Fallback: the field w.inner.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#L2OCore.extra_loss","page":"L2OCore","title":"L2OCore.extra_loss","text":"extra_loss(w::AbstractMethodWrapper, pipe, inputs, output; kwargs...) -> Real\n\nAdditional loss term contributed by a wrapper; see AbstractMethodWrapper.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#L2OCore.method_family","page":"L2OCore","title":"L2OCore.method_family","text":"method_family(method::AbstractL2OMethod)\n\nReturn PrimalFamily(), DualFamily() or PrimalDualFamily().\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#L2OCore.PrimalFamily","page":"L2OCore","title":"L2OCore.PrimalFamily","text":"PrimalFamily()\n\nValue of method_family for AbstractPrimalMethods. Family values are convenient for code that branches on what is predicted without dispatching on the method type itself (for example, data recorders).\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#L2OCore.DualFamily","page":"L2OCore","title":"L2OCore.DualFamily","text":"DualFamily()\n\nValue of method_family for AbstractDualMethods.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#L2OCore.PrimalDualFamily","page":"L2OCore","title":"L2OCore.PrimalDualFamily","text":"PrimalDualFamily()\n\nValue of method_family for AbstractPrimalDualMethods.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#L2OCore.SupervisedMSE","page":"L2OCore","title":"L2OCore.SupervisedMSE","text":"SupervisedMSE()\n\nPlain value supervision: the proxy predicts all decision variables and is trained on solver labels with the mean squared error\n\nmathcalL(θ) = frac1N n sum_i=1^N big hat g_θ(p_i) - x^star(p_i) big_2^2 \n\nthe empirical-risk-minimization baseline of the optimization-proxy literature (the value term of supervised Sobolev training). It is the primal family fallback made concrete: generate_data solves every sampled instance for primal labels, complete is the identity and loss is the supervised MSE against targets.\n\nIt needs a solver that returns (locally) optimal primal solutions for the sampled parameters and accepts any problem class, since labels are only data. A regression proxy is in general infeasible; measure its violations with evaluate.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#L2OCore.AbstractProxyModel","page":"L2OCore","title":"L2OCore.AbstractProxyModel","text":"AbstractProxyModel\n\nOptional supertype for proxy models (the learned map from parameters to raw outputs).\n\nL2OCore is framework-agnostic: any callable f(inputs::AbstractMatrix) returning an output_dimension × B array can be used as a proxy (a Flux.Chain, a closure over a Lux model and its state, a plain function). Subtyping AbstractProxyModel is only needed to attach extra behavior, for example a custom predict_batch or trainable-parameter handling.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#L2OCore.predict_batch","page":"L2OCore","title":"L2OCore.predict_batch","text":"predict_batch(proxy, inputs::AbstractMatrix)\n\nApply a proxy to a batch of inputs (input_dimension × B) and return the raw output (output_dimension × B). The fallback calls proxy(inputs); extend it for proxies that are not directly callable.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#L2OCore.AbstractProblemClass","page":"L2OCore","title":"L2OCore.AbstractProblemClass","text":"AbstractProblemClass\n\nTrait describing the mathematical class of a parametric problem for fixed parameters. Methods declare which classes they accept with supports.\n\nThe built-in classes are ordered from most to least structured: LinearConic ⊂ ConvexQuadratic (as objective classes), SmoothNonlinear and MixedInteger. Packages may add classes by subtyping AbstractProblemClass (or AbstractConvexClass) and passing the class explicitly to ParametricProblem.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#L2OCore.AbstractConvexClass","page":"L2OCore","title":"L2OCore.AbstractConvexClass","text":"AbstractConvexClass <: AbstractProblemClass\n\nProblem classes for which every instance (for fixed parameters) is a convex optimization problem, so that weak and strong duality arguments (under a constraint qualification) apply.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#L2OCore.LinearConic","page":"L2OCore","title":"L2OCore.LinearConic","text":"LinearConic()\n\nAffine objective, affine constraint functions in (products of) closed convex cones: LPs, SOCPs, SDPs, exponential-cone programs, …\n\nParameters may appear affinely in the objective and in the constraint constants.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#L2OCore.ConvexQuadratic","page":"L2OCore","title":"L2OCore.ConvexQuadratic","text":"ConvexQuadratic()\n\nConvex quadratic objective (½xᵀQx + cᵀx with Q ⪰ 0 for minimization, Q ⪯ 0 for maximization) and affine conic constraints.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#L2OCore.SmoothNonlinear","page":"L2OCore","title":"L2OCore.SmoothNonlinear","text":"SmoothNonlinear()\n\nContinuous problems with nonlinear (or nonconvex quadratic) objective or constraints, e.g. AC optimal power flow. Only first-order stationarity is meaningful; weak duality still holds but certificates require extra work.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#L2OCore.MixedInteger","page":"L2OCore","title":"L2OCore.MixedInteger","text":"MixedInteger()\n\nProblems with integrality restrictions (Integer, ZeroOne, semi-continuous or SOS constraints).\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#L2OCore.detect_problem_class","page":"L2OCore","title":"L2OCore.detect_problem_class","text":"detect_problem_class(model::JuMP.Model; atol = 1e-9) -> AbstractProblemClass\n\nInspect a JuMP model and return the most structured built-in class that describes it:\n\nany integrality/SOS/semi-continuous constraint → MixedInteger;\nany nonlinear expression, legacy @NL block or quadratic constraint with a product of two decision variables → SmoothNonlinear;\nquadratic objective with a product of decision variables → convexity is checked numerically (eigmin(sQ) ≥ -atol, s = ±1 for the sense): ConvexQuadratic if convex, SmoothNonlinear otherwise;\notherwise LinearConic.\n\nProducts parameter × variable do not change the class (for fixed parameters they are affine in the decision variables).\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#L2OCore.AbstractParametricProblem","page":"L2OCore","title":"L2OCore.AbstractParametricProblem","text":"AbstractParametricProblem\n\nSupertype of parametric problems, independent of the modeling front end. ParametricProblem is the JuMP front end; other front ends (for example an ExaModels-based one) subtype this and implement problem_class, num_parameters, num_decision_variables, num_duals, objective_sign and default_evaluator (and solve_instances to support solver-labeled data).\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#L2OCore.ParametricProblem","page":"L2OCore","title":"L2OCore.ParametricProblem","text":"ParametricProblem(model::JuMP.Model; class = detect_problem_class(model))\n\nA parametric optimization problem: a JuMP model in which the problem data that changes between instances are declared as parameters (@variable(model, p in MOI.Parameter(value))).\n\nThe wrapper fixes three layouts used throughout the ecosystem:\n\ninputs: parameters, in creation order (num_parameters × B);\nprimal: decision_variables = all non-parameter variables in creation order (num_decision_variables × B);\ndual: constraint_refs, including variable bounds but not parameter or integrality constraints; constraint i owns the rows dual_layout(problem)[i] (num_duals × B). Signs follow the MathOptInterface convention returned by JuMP.dual.\n\nclass is a trait (AbstractProblemClass) used by supports; pass it explicitly to override detection (e.g. to declare a convex QCQP or a custom class).\n\nFields are considered internal; use the accessor functions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#L2OCore.problem_class","page":"L2OCore","title":"L2OCore.problem_class","text":"problem_class(problem::ParametricProblem) -> AbstractProblemClass\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#L2OCore.parameters","page":"L2OCore","title":"L2OCore.parameters","text":"parameters(problem::ParametricProblem) -> Vector{JuMP.VariableRef}\n\nParameters of the problem, in input order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#L2OCore.decision_variables","page":"L2OCore","title":"L2OCore.decision_variables","text":"decision_variables(problem::ParametricProblem) -> Vector{JuMP.VariableRef}\n\nNon-parameter variables, in primal-prediction order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#L2OCore.constraint_refs","page":"L2OCore","title":"L2OCore.constraint_refs","text":"constraint_refs(problem::ParametricProblem) -> Vector{JuMP.ConstraintRef}\n\nConstraints (including variable bounds) in dual-prediction order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#L2OCore.num_parameters","page":"L2OCore","title":"L2OCore.num_parameters","text":"num_parameters(problem::ParametricProblem) -> Int\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#L2OCore.num_decision_variables","page":"L2OCore","title":"L2OCore.num_decision_variables","text":"num_decision_variables(problem::ParametricProblem) -> Int\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#L2OCore.num_duals","page":"L2OCore","title":"L2OCore.num_duals","text":"num_duals(problem::ParametricProblem) -> Int\n\nTotal scalar dimension of all constraints in constraint_refs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#L2OCore.dual_layout","page":"L2OCore","title":"L2OCore.dual_layout","text":"dual_layout(problem::ParametricProblem) -> Vector{UnitRange{Int}}\n\nRows of a dual prediction owned by each constraint in constraint_refs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#L2OCore.parameter_values","page":"L2OCore","title":"L2OCore.parameter_values","text":"parameter_values(problem::ParametricProblem) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nCurrent values of the parameters stored in the JuMP model (a single instance).\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#L2OCore.objective_sign","page":"L2OCore","title":"L2OCore.objective_sign","text":"objective_sign(problem) -> Int\n\n1 for minimization (and feasibility) problems, -1 for maximization; objective_sign(problem) * objective is always to be minimized.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#L2OCore.supports","page":"L2OCore","title":"L2OCore.supports","text":"supports(method::AbstractL2OMethod, class::AbstractProblemClass) -> Bool\n\nWhether method is defined for problems of class, i.e. whether the mathematical assumptions of the method hold for that class. The fallback is false: a method must opt in explicitly, class by class, e.g.\n\nL2OCore.supports(::MyMethod, ::L2OCore.AbstractConvexClass) = true\n\nThere is deliberately no silent fallback that would run a method on a class its derivation does not cover; see check_supports.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#L2OCore.check_supports","page":"L2OCore","title":"L2OCore.check_supports","text":"check_supports(problem::ParametricProblem, method::AbstractL2OMethod)\n\nThrow an UnsupportedProblemError unless supports(method, problem_class(problem)). Called by L2OPipeline before anything else.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#L2OCore.UnsupportedProblemError","page":"L2OCore","title":"L2OCore.UnsupportedProblemError","text":"UnsupportedProblemError(method, class)\n\nThrown by check_supports when a method is used on a problem class it does not support.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#L2OCore.AbstractEvaluator","page":"L2OCore","title":"L2OCore.AbstractEvaluator","text":"AbstractEvaluator\n\nBatch evaluation backend for a ParametricProblem. Evaluators turn batches of parameters P (num_parameters × B), primal points X (num_decision_variables × B) and dual points Y (num_duals × B) into objective values, constraint violations and dual bounds.\n\nBuilt-in backends:\n\nMatrixEvaluator: extracts A, Q, c, … once and evaluates with array operations (vectorized, differentiable by array AD, element-type generic). Used for LinearConic and ConvexQuadratic problems whose parameters enter affinely.\nJuMPEvaluator: per-instance fallback through JuMP.value; works for any expression JuMP can evaluate (including nonlinear) but is not meant to be differentiated.\n\nExtension point. Kernel packages (e.g. an ExaModels/BatchNLPKernels extension) add a backend by subtyping AbstractEvaluator and implementing\n\nfunction required returns\nbatch_objective(ev, X, P) yes length-B vector\nconstraint_values(ev, X, P) yes num_duals × B\nrow_violation(ev, X, P) yes num_duals × B, ≥ 0\nequality_rows(ev) yes BitVector of length num_duals\ncone_violation(ev, X, P) if non-decomposable cones n_cones × B, ≥ 0\ndual_bound(ev, Y, P) for dual methods (bound, infeasibility) vectors\n\nand, optionally, a method of default_evaluator for the problems it should handle.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#L2OCore.MatrixEvaluator","page":"L2OCore","title":"L2OCore.MatrixEvaluator","text":"MatrixEvaluator(problem::ParametricProblem; T = Float64)\n\nMatrix-form evaluator for problems of the form (for each instance p)\n\nmin_x  tfrac12 x^top Q x + c^top x + x^top H p + c_p^top p + tfrac12 p^top Q_pp p + c_0\nquad textst quad A x + B p + b in mathcalK\n\nwhere 𝒦 is the product of the constraint sets in dual_layout order (max is handled through the sense). Box-like sets become row bounds l ≤ Ax + Bp + b ≤ u; other cones are kept as blocks.\n\nAll batch operations are matrix products and broadcasts, so they are vectorized, allocation-light and differentiable with array AD (Zygote, Enzyme, …). cone_violation and the cone part of dual_bound evaluate MOI.Utilities.distance_to_set per column (differentiable with Zygote for the second-order cone; not GPU-friendly).\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#L2OCore.JuMPEvaluator","page":"L2OCore","title":"L2OCore.JuMPEvaluator","text":"JuMPEvaluator(problem::ParametricProblem)\n\nReference evaluator that loops over the batch and evaluates every expression with JuMP.value(var_value, expr). It supports anything JuMP can evaluate (affine, quadratic and GenericNonlinearExpr functions, any MOI set with MOI.Utilities.distance_to_set) and serves as the ground truth against which faster backends are tested. It is not meant for training loops.\n\ndual_bound is not available for this backend.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#L2OCore.default_evaluator","page":"L2OCore","title":"L2OCore.default_evaluator","text":"default_evaluator(problem::ParametricProblem; T = Float64) -> AbstractEvaluator\n\nMatrixEvaluator when the problem can be put in matrix form (affine constraints, at most quadratic objective, parameters entering affinely), otherwise JuMPEvaluator. The result is cached in the problem.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#L2OCore.has_matrix_form","page":"L2OCore","title":"L2OCore.has_matrix_form","text":"has_matrix_form(problem::ParametricProblem) -> Bool\n\nWhether MatrixEvaluator can represent problem: affine constraint functions (parameters may appear in the constants), at most quadratic objective, no legacy @NL block.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#L2OCore.batch_objective","page":"L2OCore","title":"L2OCore.batch_objective","text":"batch_objective(ev::AbstractEvaluator, X, P) -> AbstractVector\n\nObjective value of each column of X (with parameters the matching column of P), in the sense of the model (maximization objectives are not negated).\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#L2OCore.constraint_values","page":"L2OCore","title":"L2OCore.constraint_values","text":"constraint_values(ev::AbstractEvaluator, X, P) -> AbstractMatrix\n\nValue of every constraint function, stacked in dual_layout order (num_duals × B).\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#L2OCore.row_violation","page":"L2OCore","title":"L2OCore.row_violation","text":"row_violation(ev::AbstractEvaluator, X, P) -> AbstractMatrix\n\nElement-wise violation max(l - f, 0) + max(f - u, 0) of every row whose set is a box (EqualTo, GreaterThan, LessThan, Interval, Zeros, Nonnegatives, Nonpositives), and 0 for rows of non-decomposable cones (see cone_violation). Shape num_duals × B.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#L2OCore.cone_violation","page":"L2OCore","title":"L2OCore.cone_violation","text":"cone_violation(ev::AbstractEvaluator, X, P) -> AbstractMatrix\n\nEuclidean distance of each non-decomposable conic constraint (second-order, PSD, exponential, …) to its set, n_cones × B. The fallback returns a 0 × B matrix.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#L2OCore.equality_rows","page":"L2OCore","title":"L2OCore.equality_rows","text":"equality_rows(ev::AbstractEvaluator) -> BitVector\n\nMask of rows (in dual_layout order) that are equality constraints.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#L2OCore.bound_rows","page":"L2OCore","title":"L2OCore.bound_rows","text":"bound_rows(ev::AbstractEvaluator) -> BitVector\n\nMask of rows that are variable bounds (VariableRef-in-set constraints).\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#L2OCore.dual_bound","page":"L2OCore","title":"L2OCore.dual_bound","text":"dual_bound(ev::AbstractEvaluator, Y, P; atol = 1e-6) -> (bound, infeasibility)\n\nValue of the Lagrangian dual function at each column of Y (signs as returned by JuMP.dual) and the dual infeasibility of that column.\n\nBy weak duality, whenever infeasibility[k] ≤ atol, bound[k] is a lower bound (upper bound for maximization) on the optimal value of instance k, up to infeasibility[k] * ‖x⋆‖₁ when the stationarity residual is nonzero (see the Theory page). Columns that are not dual feasible get bound = -Inf (+Inf for maximization).\n\nDual signs follow JuMP.dual (the MathOptInterface convention). For quadratic objectives sQ ⪰ 0 is required and verified; otherwise no bound is returned.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#L2OCore.EvaluationReport","page":"L2OCore","title":"L2OCore.EvaluationReport","text":"EvaluationReport\n\nPer-instance quality metrics returned by evaluate. Every field is a length-B vector or nothing when it does not apply.\n\nobjective: primal objective of the prediction;\nmax_violation: largest violation over all constraints;\nmax_equality_violation, max_inequality_violation;\ndual_bound, dual_infeasibility: see dual_bound;\ngap: |objective - dual_bound| / max(1, |objective|) when both a primal and a feasible dual prediction are available.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#L2OCore.evaluate","page":"L2OCore","title":"L2OCore.evaluate","text":"evaluate(pipe::L2OPipeline, inputs, prediction; atol = 1e-6)\nevaluate(problem::ParametricProblem, inputs, prediction;\n         evaluator = default_evaluator(problem), atol = 1e-6)\n\nQuality metrics of a batch of completed predictions as an EvaluationReport: objective and violations of the primal part, dual bound and dual infeasibility of the dual part, and the gap when both exist.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#L2OCore.AbstractParameterSampler","page":"L2OCore","title":"L2OCore.AbstractParameterSampler","text":"AbstractParameterSampler\n\nDistribution of problem parameters. Implement sample_parameters(sampler, rng, n) returning a num_parameters × n matrix. Any function f(rng, n) can be used directly as a sampler.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#L2OCore.sample_parameters","page":"L2OCore","title":"L2OCore.sample_parameters","text":"sample_parameters(sampler, rng::Random.AbstractRNG, n::Integer) -> AbstractMatrix\n\nDraw n parameter vectors, one per column.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#L2OCore.BoxSampler","page":"L2OCore","title":"L2OCore.BoxSampler","text":"BoxSampler(lower, upper)\n\nIndependent uniform parameters, p[i] ~ U(lower[i], upper[i]).\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#L2OCore.ScaledSampler","page":"L2OCore","title":"L2OCore.ScaledSampler","text":"ScaledSampler(nominal; low = 0.8, high = 1.2)\nScaledSampler(problem::ParametricProblem; low = 0.8, high = 1.2)\n\nMultiplicative perturbation of a nominal parameter vector, p[i] = nominal[i] * U(low, high). With a problem, the nominal values are the current parameter values of the JuMP model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#L2OCore.L2ODataset","page":"L2OCore","title":"L2OCore.L2ODataset","text":"L2ODataset\n\nColumns-as-samples dataset (N samples):\n\ninputs: num_parameters × N (always present);\nprimal: num_decision_variables × N or nothing;\ndual: num_duals × N or nothing;\nobjective: length N or nothing;\nstatus: termination status per sample or nothing (unsolved data, e.g. for self-supervised methods);\nderivatives: an AbstractDerivativeLabels or nothing;\nmetadata: free-form Dict{String,Any} (problem id, sampler, solver, tolerances…), mirrored by the dataset-card conventions in the design docs.\n\nSamples whose solve failed hold NaN labels; filter them with ds[findall(ok)] where ok comes from status.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#L2OCore.num_samples","page":"L2OCore","title":"L2OCore.num_samples","text":"num_samples(ds::L2ODataset) -> Int\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#L2OCore.with_derivatives","page":"L2OCore","title":"L2OCore.with_derivatives","text":"with_derivatives(ds::L2ODataset, labels::Union{Nothing,AbstractDerivativeLabels}) -> L2ODataset\n\nCopy of ds with its derivative labels replaced (datasets are immutable).\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#L2OCore.AbstractDerivativeLabels","page":"L2OCore","title":"L2OCore.AbstractDerivativeLabels","text":"AbstractDerivativeLabels\n\nOptional derivative (sensitivity) labels attached to a dataset, e.g. computed by differentiating through the solver. Datasets never require them; methods that use them (Sobolev-type training) check for their presence.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#L2OCore.JacobianLabels","page":"L2OCore","title":"L2OCore.JacobianLabels","text":"JacobianLabels(J)\n\nFull Jacobians of the primal solution with respect to the parameters: J is num_decision_variables × num_parameters × N.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#L2OCore.PullbackLabels","page":"L2OCore","title":"L2OCore.PullbackLabels","text":"PullbackLabels(seeds, values)\n\nVector–Jacobian products: for each sample k, values[:, k] = J_kᵀ seeds[:, k] where J_k = ∂x*/∂p; seeds is num_decision_variables × N, values is num_parameters × N. Cheaper than full Jacobians and sufficient for pullback-based Sobolev losses.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#L2OCore.PushforwardLabels","page":"L2OCore","title":"L2OCore.PushforwardLabels","text":"PushforwardLabels(directions, values)\n\nJacobian–vector products: for each sample k, values[:, k] = J_k directions[:, k] where J_k = ∂x*/∂p; directions is num_parameters × N, values is num_decision_variables × N. The forward-mode counterpart of PullbackLabels: cheaper than full Jacobians when the number of parameters is large, and sufficient for directional Sobolev losses.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#L2OCore.solve_instances","page":"L2OCore","title":"L2OCore.solve_instances","text":"solve_instances(problem, inputs; optimizer = nothing, primal = true,\n                dual = false, silent = true) -> L2ODataset\n\nSolve one instance per column of inputs by setting the parameter values of the JuMP model and calling optimize!. Records the objective value, termination status and, as requested, the primal solution (decision_variables order) and the dual solution (dual_layout order, JuMP.dual signs).\n\nThe JuMP model is modified in place (parameter values, optimizer).\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#L2OCore.Prediction","page":"L2OCore","title":"L2OCore.Prediction","text":"Prediction(; primal = nothing, dual = nothing)\n\nA batch of (completed) predictions: primal is num_decision_variables × B in decision_variables order, dual is num_duals × B in dual_layout order. Either may be nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#L2OCore.AbstractDecomposition","page":"L2OCore","title":"L2OCore.AbstractDecomposition","text":"AbstractDecomposition\n\nMethod-specific structure computed once per problem by decompose: which quantities the proxy predicts and how the rest is recovered by complete. Examples: DLL's split of the duals into predicted y and completed z; DC3's independent/dependent variables. A decomposition must implement output_dimension.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#L2OCore.FullSpaceDecomposition","page":"L2OCore","title":"L2OCore.FullSpaceDecomposition","text":"FullSpaceDecomposition(primal::Bool, dual::Bool)\n\nThe family fallback: the proxy predicts all primal variables (if primal) followed by all duals (if dual), stacked vertically, and completion is the identity.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#L2OCore.L2OPipeline","page":"L2OCore","title":"L2OCore.L2OPipeline","text":"L2OPipeline(problem::ParametricProblem, method::AbstractL2OMethod;\n            evaluator = default_evaluator(problem))\n\nBind a method to a problem: checks supports (throwing UnsupportedProblemError), computes decompose once and keeps an AbstractEvaluator. All pipeline steps take the pipeline:\n\npipe = L2OPipeline(problem, method)\ndata = generate_data(pipe; sampler, n = 100, optimizer)\nℓ    = loss(pipe, data.inputs, proxy(data.inputs))\npred = complete(pipe, inputs, proxy(inputs))\nevaluate(pipe, inputs, pred)\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#L2OCore.decompose","page":"L2OCore","title":"L2OCore.decompose","text":"decompose(problem::ParametricProblem, method::AbstractL2OMethod) -> AbstractDecomposition\n\nCompute the method-specific structure of problem. Called once by L2OPipeline after check_supports.\n\nFallback by family (FullSpaceDecomposition): primal methods predict all primal variables, dual methods all duals, primal-dual methods both.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#L2OCore.input_dimension","page":"L2OCore","title":"L2OCore.input_dimension","text":"input_dimension(pipe::L2OPipeline) -> Int\n\nNumber of rows of the proxy input; the fallback is num_parameters.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#L2OCore.output_dimension","page":"L2OCore","title":"L2OCore.output_dimension","text":"output_dimension(decomposition::AbstractDecomposition) -> Int\noutput_dimension(pipe::L2OPipeline) -> Int\n\nNumber of rows the proxy must output.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#L2OCore.generate_data","page":"L2OCore","title":"L2OCore.generate_data","text":"generate_data(pipe::L2OPipeline; sampler, n, rng = Random.default_rng(), kwargs...)\ngenerate_data(method, pipe; sampler, n, rng, kwargs...)  # extension point\n\nBuild an L2ODataset for training pipe.method.\n\nFamily fallback: sample n parameter vectors from sampler and solve every instance with solve_instances (kwargs such as optimizer are forwarded), recording primal labels for primal methods, dual labels for dual methods and both for primal-dual methods. Methods override the second form, e.g. self-supervised methods that need only inputs, or DC3 storing only independent variables.\n\n\n\n\n\ngenerate_data(::PenaltyMethod, pipe; sampler, n, rng, optimizer = nothing)\n\nPenalty training is self-supervised: only parameters are sampled. When an optimizer is given, instances are also solved so that the dataset carries reference optima for evaluation (objective gaps, dual bounds).\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#L2OCore.complete","page":"L2OCore","title":"L2OCore.complete","text":"complete(pipe::L2OPipeline, inputs, output) -> Prediction\ncomplete(method, pipe, inputs, output)  # extension point\n\nTurn the raw proxy output (output_dimension × B) into a Prediction in the method's own sense (projection, equality completion, dual completion, repair…). The FullSpaceDecomposition fallback only splits the output into primal and dual blocks.\n\nImplementations should be written with array operations so they can be differentiated and run on accelerators when the loss needs them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#L2OCore.loss","page":"L2OCore","title":"L2OCore.loss","text":"loss(pipe::L2OPipeline, inputs, output; targets = nothing) -> Real\nloss(method, pipe, inputs, output; targets)  # extension point\n\nTraining loss of a batch of raw proxy outputs. Training loops should also pass proxy = <the proxy> so that loss terms which differentiate the proxy with respect to its inputs (Sobolev / derivative supervision) can use it; methods that do not need it ignore it. Fallback: supervised mean squared error between the completed prediction and the labels in targets (an L2ODataset restricted to the batch, or a Prediction). Self-supervised methods override it (see the penalty method in LearningToOptimize.jl).\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#L2OCore.train_proxy!","page":"L2OCore","title":"L2OCore.train_proxy!","text":"train_proxy!(method, pipe::L2OPipeline, proxy, data; kwargs...)\n\nTrain proxy in place for method. L2OCore defines no method for it, since training depends on the ML framework; framework-specific packages (e.g. LearningToOptimize.jl for Flux) and method packages add methods.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#L2OCore.BoundedOutput","page":"L2OCore","title":"L2OCore.BoundedOutput","text":"BoundedOutput(inner::AbstractL2OMethod; mode = :smooth)\n\nWrapper that maps the primal part of the raw proxy output into the variable bounds l  x  u before the inner method sees it, both in loss and in complete — a bound-preserving output layer.\n\nWith mode = :smooth each coordinate uses\n\nx_j = begincases\nl_j + (u_j - l_j)σ(z_j)  l_j u_j text finite\nl_j + operatornamesoftplus(z_j)  textonly  l_j text finite\nu_j - operatornamesoftplus(-z_j)  textonly  u_j text finite\nz_j  textfree\nendcases\n\n(fixed variables are set to their value), which is smooth and never has a zero gradient; mode = :clamp uses the Euclidean projection onto the box, x = min(max(z l) u), whose gradient vanishes outside the box.\n\nThe inner method must use a FullSpaceDecomposition with primal predictions (its raw output starts with all primal variables); the bounds are those declared on the JuMP variables.\n\nEvery completed primal prediction satisfies the variable bounds. Dual outputs are not modified, so a dual bound of the inner method is unchanged; other primal properties of the inner method (e.g. satisfied equalities) can be lost, since changing x can break them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#L2OCore.bound_transform","page":"L2OCore","title":"L2OCore.bound_transform","text":"bound_transform(d::BoundedOutputDecomposition, Z::AbstractMatrix, mode::Symbol)\n\nApply the bound-preserving map of BoundedOutput to the primal block Z (num_decision_variables × B). Non-mutating and differentiable with array AD.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#L2OCore.BoundedOutputDecomposition","page":"L2OCore","title":"L2OCore.BoundedOutputDecomposition","text":"BoundedOutputDecomposition\n\nDecomposition of a BoundedOutput pipeline: the inner decomposition and the variable bounds.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#L2OCore.ProjectionRepair","page":"L2OCore","title":"L2OCore.ProjectionRepair","text":"ProjectionRepair(inner::AbstractL2OMethod, optimizer)\n\nWrapper that repairs the primal prediction of inner at inference time by the Euclidean projection onto the feasible set mathcal F(p) of each instance,\n\nΠ_mathcal F(p)(hat x) = argmin_x in mathcal F(p)  x - hat x_2^2\n\nsolved with optimizer (any JuMP solver for the resulting convex QP or conic program). Training is unchanged: loss is the inner loss.\n\nThe problem class must be convex (so mathcal F(p) is closed and convex and the projection is unique) and the feasible set of each instance non-empty. The repaired prediction is then feasible to the solver's tolerance, and dual outputs pass through unchanged. The projection is nonexpansive: for every feasible x, in particular an optimal x^star, Π(hat x) - x le hat x - x. The objective value need not improve.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#L2OCore.project_onto_feasible_set","page":"L2OCore","title":"L2OCore.project_onto_feasible_set","text":"project_onto_feasible_set(d::ProjectionDecomposition, inputs, X) -> Matrix\n\nEuclidean projection of each column of X onto the feasible set of the matching instance. Columns whose projection problem is not solved to optimality are returned as NaN.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"api_l2ocore/#L2OCore.ProjectionDecomposition","page":"L2OCore","title":"L2OCore.ProjectionDecomposition","text":"ProjectionDecomposition\n\nDecomposition of a ProjectionRepair pipeline: the inner decomposition and a copy of the JuMP model used for projections.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"#LearningToOptimize.jl","page":"Home","title":"LearningToOptimize.jl","text":"LearningToOptimize.jl trains optimization proxies: machine-learning models that map the parameters of a parametric optimization problem, written in JuMP, to (near-)optimal primal and/or dual solutions, orders of magnitude faster than a solver. It provides the shared layer that every learning-to-optimize (L2O) method needs — a problem wrapper with fixed input/output layouts, dataset generation, batched evaluation of objectives, violations and dual bounds — so that a new method only implements what is specific to it. The reference method shipped here is the penalty (\"soft loss\") approach, eq. (2) of Donti, Rolnick & Kolter, ICLR 2021; methods with completion steps (feasibility restoration, dual completion) live in sibling packages. See the survey by Kotary et al. (IJCAI 2021) for the field, and our Hugging Face organization for datasets, pre-trained models and benchmarks.\n\nflowchart LR\n    M[\"JuMP model<br/>with parameters\"] --> PP[\"ParametricProblem\"]\n    PP --> PIPE[\"L2OPipeline(problem, method)\"]\n    PIPE --> D[\"generate_data\"]\n    D --> T[\"train proxy\"]\n    T --> C[\"complete\"]\n    C --> E[\"evaluate: objective,<br/>violations, dual bound\"]","category":"section"},{"location":"#Installation","page":"Home","title":"Installation","text":"The API described here is on the development version. The registered LearningToOptimize 1.0.0 predates it (it has no L2OCore, ParametricProblem or L2OPipeline). Until the next release, install the core subpackage first and then the main package, on any Julia version from 1.10:\n\nusing Pkg\nPkg.add(url = \"https://github.com/LearningToOptimize/LearningToOptimize.jl\", subdir = \"lib/L2OCore\")\nPkg.add(url = \"https://github.com/LearningToOptimize/LearningToOptimize.jl\")\n\nThe example below also needs JuMP, HiGHS and Flux: Pkg.add([\"JuMP\", \"HiGHS\", \"Flux\"]).","category":"section"},{"location":"#Minimal-example","page":"Home","title":"Minimal example","text":"using LearningToOptimize, JuMP, HiGHS, Flux, Random\n\nmodel = Model()\n@variable(model, d[1:2] in MOI.Parameter.([1.0, 2.0]))   # parameters\n@variable(model, 0 <= g[1:3] <= 4)\n@constraint(model, g[1] + g[2] == d[1])\n@constraint(model, g[3] == d[2])\n@objective(model, Min, g[1] + 3g[2] + 2g[3])\n\nproblem = ParametricProblem(model)                       # detects LinearConic\npipe = L2OPipeline(problem, PenaltyMethod())\nsampler = BoxSampler([0.5, 1.0], [1.5, 2.0])\ntrain = generate_data(pipe; sampler, n = 128)            # self-supervised: parameters only\ntest = generate_data(pipe; sampler, n = 32, optimizer = HiGHS.Optimizer)\n\nproxy = Flux.f64(Chain(Dense(2 => 32, relu), Dense(32 => 3)))\ntrain_flux!(pipe, proxy, train; epochs = 200, optimiser = Flux.Optimisers.Adam(1e-2))\n\nprediction = complete(pipe, test.inputs, proxy(test.inputs))\nevaluate(pipe, test.inputs, prediction)                  # objective, violations, gaps","category":"section"},{"location":"#What-you-need-to-provide","page":"Home","title":"What you need to provide","text":"A JuMP model whose instance data are parameters (@variable(model, p in MOI.Parameter(value))).\nA parameter distribution: BoxSampler, ScaledSampler, or any function (rng, n) -> matrix.\nA proxy: any callable mapping a num_parameters × batch matrix to the method's output (Flux, Lux, plain Julia).\nA solver, only for methods (or evaluations) that need labels or reference optima.","category":"section"},{"location":"#Where-to-go-next","page":"Home","title":"Where to go next","text":"Theory: notation, batched evaluation, the Lagrangian dual bound and the penalty method.\nDesign: how the ecosystem is organized (lib/L2OCore, method packages), the type hierarchy, data flow and method lifecycle.\nGetting started: train a penalty proxy for a small LP in seconds.\nAdding a method: what a method package implements.\nDataset generation with solver labels: ProblemIterator, recorders, samplers, and training Flux or MLJ models on the saved files.","category":"section"},{"location":"#The-LearningToOptimize-ecosystem","page":"Home","title":"The LearningToOptimize ecosystem","text":"Package What it provides\nLearningToOptimize.jl the penalty method, Flux training and solver-labeled dataset generation; hosts the two subpackages below\n↳ L2OCore the shared interface: method families, problem wrapper, batched evaluators, datasets\n↳ L2OSobolev derivative (Sobolev) supervision added to any method\nL2ODLL.jl Dual Lagrangian Learning: dual proxies that give valid dual bounds for conic problems\nL2ODC3.jl DC3: equality completion and inequality correction for primal proxies\nL2OALM.jl primal-dual learning with an augmented Lagrangian\n\n","category":"section"},{"location":"datasetgen/#Dataset-generation-with-solver-labels","page":"Dataset generation: solver labels","title":"Dataset generation with solver labels","text":"Besides the method-dispatched generate_data, LearningToOptimize writes datasets of solver solutions to CSV or Arrow files. The workflow has three parts: a ProblemIterator holds the parameter values of every instance, a Recorder chooses what to save, and solve_batch solves every instance and writes one row per successful solve. Every instance gets a UUID, which links input and output files.\n\n(Image: Dataset generation workflow)\n\nThe code on this page runs as one script in an environment with LearningToOptimize, JuMP, HiGHS, CSV, DataFrames, Flux, MLJ, MLJFlux and Optimisers.","category":"section"},{"location":"datasetgen/#The-problem-iterator","page":"Dataset generation: solver labels","title":"The problem iterator","text":"using LearningToOptimize, JuMP, HiGHS\n\nmodel = Model(HiGHS.Optimizer)\nset_silent(model)\n@variable(model, x)\np = @variable(model, p in MOI.Parameter(1.0))\n@constraint(model, cons, x + p >= 3)\n@objective(model, Min, 2x)\n\nparameter_values = Dict(p => collect(1.0:10.0))\nproblem_iterator = ProblemIterator(parameter_values)\n\nSave the parameter values of the iterator with\n\nsave(problem_iterator, \"input_file\", CSVFile)\n\nwhich writes input_file.csv:\n\nid p\n1d08db14-… 1.0\n1d08db70-… 2.0\n… …\n1d08dc2e-… 10.0\n\nload(\"input_file\", CSVFile) reads the file back as a DataFrame.","category":"section"},{"location":"datasetgen/#Samplers","page":"Dataset generation: solver labels","title":"Samplers","text":"Instead of listing parameter values by hand, sample them around the current values of the parameters with the predefined samplers (box_sampler, line_sampler, scaled_distribution_sampler) or with your own function that takes the vector of parameters and returns a matrix with one row per parameter and one column per instance. general_sampler combines several samplers. Given a model file that JuMP's read_from_file can read (tested with MathOptFormat), it samples the parameters and saves them:\n\nwrite_to_file(model, \"model.mof.json\")\nsampled_iterator = general_sampler(\n    \"model.mof.json\";\n    samplers = [\n        (original_parameters) -> scaled_distribution_sampler(original_parameters, 100),\n        (original_parameters) -> line_sampler(original_parameters, 1.01:0.01:1.25),\n        (original_parameters) -> box_sampler(original_parameters, 30),\n    ],\n    save_file = \"sampled_input\",\n    filetype = CSVFile,\n)\n\nThe result is a ProblemIterator whose parameter values are also in sampled_input.csv.","category":"section"},{"location":"datasetgen/#The-recorder","page":"Dataset generation: solver labels","title":"The recorder","text":"Choose what to record and solve every instance:\n\nrecorder = Recorder{CSVFile}(\"output_file\"; primal_variables = [x], dual_variables = [cons])\nsolve_batch(problem_iterator, recorder)\n\nThis writes output_file.csv, one row per successful solve:\n\nid x dual_cons objective time status primal_status dual_status\n1d08db14-… 2.0 2.0 4.0 0.0002 OPTIMAL FEASIBLE_POINT FEASIBLE_POINT\n1d08db70-… 1.0 2.0 2.0 0.0001 OPTIMAL FEASIBLE_POINT FEASIBLE_POINT\n… … … … … … … …\n\nTo write Arrow files instead, use Recorder{ArrowFile}; see Reading from and compressing Arrow files.","category":"section"},{"location":"datasetgen/#Training-a-proxy-on-the-saved-data","page":"Dataset generation: solver labels","title":"Training a proxy on the saved data","text":"With Flux, join inputs and outputs on the instance id (only successful solves have an output row) and train in Flux's explicit style:\n\nusing CSV, DataFrames, Flux\n\ninput_data = CSV.read(\"input_file.csv\", DataFrame)\noutput_data = CSV.read(\"output_file.csv\", DataFrame)\ndata = innerjoin(input_data, output_data; on = :id)\n\nX = Matrix{Float32}(data[!, [:p]])'             # parameters × instances\nY = Matrix{Float32}(data[!, [:x, :dual_cons]])' # predicted solution × instances\n\nnn = Chain(Dense(size(X, 1) => 64, relu), Dense(64 => 32, relu), Dense(32 => size(Y, 1)))\nopt_state = Flux.setup(Flux.Adam(1e-2), nn)\nfor epoch = 1:200\n    Flux.train!((m, x, y) -> Flux.mse(m(x), y), nn, [(X, Y)], opt_state)\nend\npredictions = nn(X)\n\nWith MLJ, use the MultitargetNeuralNetworkRegressor of MLJFlux and the FullyConnectedBuilder of this package:\n\nusing MLJ, MLJFlux, Optimisers\n\ninput_features = data[!, [:p]]\noutput_variables = data[!, [:x, :dual_cons]]\nregressor = MultitargetNeuralNetworkRegressor(;\n    builder = FullyConnectedBuilder([64, 32]),\n    rng = 123,\n    epochs = 20,\n    optimiser = Optimisers.Adam(),\n)\nmach = machine(regressor, input_features, output_variables)\nfit!(mach; verbosity = 0)\npredict(mach, input_features)","category":"section"},{"location":"datasetgen/#Evaluating-the-proxy","page":"Dataset generation: solver labels","title":"Evaluating the proxy","text":"general_evaluator evaluates a trained MLJ machine on the instances of a ProblemIterator. It returns a NamedTuple with the objective value and the infeasibility of the predicted solution for each instance, and the total inference time and allocated memory.\n\nevaluation = general_evaluator(problem_iterator, mach)\n\n","category":"section"},{"location":"examples/penalty_lp/#Getting-started:-a-penalty-proxy-for-a-parametric-LP","page":"Getting started","title":"Getting started: a penalty proxy for a parametric LP","text":"This example trains a small neural network to predict solutions of a parametric economic-dispatch LP, using the self-supervised PenaltyMethod (no solver needed for training), and evaluates it against solver optima. It runs on a CPU in a few seconds.\n\nusing LearningToOptimize\nusing JuMP, HiGHS, Flux, Random","category":"section"},{"location":"examples/penalty_lp/#The-parametric-problem","page":"Getting started","title":"The parametric problem","text":"Three generators with costs 1, 3 and 2 serve two demands d (parameters); capacities are variable bounds.\n\nmodel = Model()\n@variable(model, d[1:2] in MOI.Parameter.([1.0, 2.0]))\n@variable(model, 0 <= g[1:3] <= 4)\n@constraint(model, g[1] + g[2] == d[1])\n@constraint(model, g[3] == d[2])\n@objective(model, Min, g[1] + 3g[2] + 2g[3])\n\nproblem = ParametricProblem(model)\nParametricProblem{LinearConic}: 2 parameters, 3 variables, 8 constraints (8 dual rows)\n\nThe problem class is detected from the model and the layouts are fixed:\n\nproblem_class(problem),\nnum_parameters(problem),\nnum_decision_variables(problem),\nnum_duals(problem)\n(LinearConic(), 2, 3, 8)","category":"section"},{"location":"examples/penalty_lp/#Method-and-pipeline","page":"Getting started","title":"Method and pipeline","text":"L2OPipeline checks that the method supports the problem class and prepares the evaluator used by the loss.\n\nmethod = PenaltyMethod(; ρ_eq = 20.0, ρ_ineq = 20.0)\npipe = L2OPipeline(problem, method)\nL2OPipeline(PenaltyMethod on ParametricProblem{LinearConic}: 2 parameters, 3 variables, 8 constraints (8 dual rows); FullSpaceDecomposition, MatrixEvaluator)","category":"section"},{"location":"examples/penalty_lp/#Data","page":"Getting started","title":"Data","text":"Training data are only parameter samples; a smaller test set is solved with HiGHS for reference.\n\nrng = Random.MersenneTwister(1)\nsampler = BoxSampler([0.5, 1.0], [1.5, 2.0])\ntrain = generate_data(pipe; sampler, n = 128, rng)\ntest = generate_data(pipe; sampler, n = 32, rng, optimizer = HiGHS.Optimizer)\nL2ODataset{Float64}: 32 samples; labels: primal, objective, status","category":"section"},{"location":"examples/penalty_lp/#Training","page":"Getting started","title":"Training","text":"proxy = Flux.f64(Chain(Dense(2 => 32, relu), Dense(32 => 3)))\nhistory = train_flux!(\n    pipe,\n    proxy,\n    train;\n    epochs = 200,\n    batchsize = 32,\n    rng,\n    optimiser = Flux.Optimisers.Adam(1e-2),\n)\nhistory[[1, 50, 100, 200]]\n4-element Vector{Float64}:\n 52.10454158093626\n  4.1584206212643275\n  3.9555027662088778\n  3.939194178732034","category":"section"},{"location":"examples/penalty_lp/#Evaluation","page":"Getting started","title":"Evaluation","text":"prediction = complete(pipe, test.inputs, proxy(test.inputs))\nreport = evaluate(pipe, test.inputs, prediction)\nEvaluationReport\n  objective                 mean = 3.7110553568991413  (32 instances)\n  max_violation             mean = 0.051209629348341765  (32 instances)\n  max_equality_violation    mean = 0.04960461124269049  (32 instances)\n  max_inequality_violation  mean = 0.05032840143614145  (32 instances)\n\n\nMean absolute optimality gap and worst constraint violation:\n\nsum(abs.(report.objective .- test.objective)) / 32, maximum(report.max_violation)\n(0.22500672402337465, 0.05267098645124482)\n\nThe penalty loss only discourages violations, so the proxy lands close to, but not exactly on, the feasible set. The report separates equality and inequality (here: capacity bound) violations:\n\nmaximum(report.max_equality_violation), maximum(report.max_inequality_violation)\n(0.05220696866451102, 0.05267098645124482)\n\nFraction of test instances whose worst violation is below 1e-2 (often none at this training budget). Methods with a completion step (DC3, DLL, …) are the ones that restore feasibility or dual feasibility exactly.\n\ncount(<=(1e-2), report.max_violation) / 32\n0.0","category":"section"},{"location":"examples/penalty_lp/#Repair-layers","page":"Getting started","title":"Repair layers","text":"Repair layers wrap any primal method. BoundedOutput maps the network output into the variable bounds during training and inference, so the bounds hold by construction. Here the only inequalities are the capacity bounds, so their largest violation is exactly zero:\n\nbounded = BoundedOutput(method)\nbpipe = L2OPipeline(problem, bounded)\nbproxy = Flux.f64(Chain(Dense(2 => 32, relu), Dense(32 => 3)))\ntrain_flux!(\n    bpipe,\n    bproxy,\n    train;\n    epochs = 200,\n    batchsize = 32,\n    rng,\n    optimiser = Flux.Optimisers.Adam(1e-2),\n)\nbpred = complete(bpipe, test.inputs, bproxy(test.inputs))\nmaximum(evaluate(pipe, test.inputs, bpred).max_inequality_violation)\n0.0\n\nProjectionRepair projects the prediction onto the feasible set of each instance at inference time (a small QP per instance). The result is feasible up to the solver tolerance, and since the projection onto a convex set is nonexpansive it is never farther from the optimal solution than the raw prediction:\n\nrepaired = L2OPipeline(problem, ProjectionRepair(bounded, HiGHS.Optimizer))\nrpred = complete(repaired, test.inputs, bproxy(test.inputs))\nmaximum(evaluate(pipe, test.inputs, rpred).max_violation)\n0.0\n\nMean optimality gap before and after the repair:\n\ngap(pred) = sum(abs.(evaluate(pipe, test.inputs, pred).objective .- test.objective)) / 32\ngap(bpred), gap(rpred)\n(0.11964507889815269, 0.025676329874660828)\n\n","category":"section"}]
}
